(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.ffe4789c16.js"]={"6":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"In prion disease, the normal cellular prion protein becomes the pathogenic scrapie isoform through which change?","choices":{"A":"Proteolytic cleavage of the PrP","B":"Beta-sheet conformational shift","C":"Acquisition of a nucleic acid","D":"Loss of membrane anchoring","E":"Excess PrP gene transcription"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Normal PrPc, a 30-kDa protein rich in alpha-helix, undergoes a post-translational change in shape into PrPSc, an isoform rich in beta-pleated sheet. Proteolytic cleavage does not create the pathogenic form; PrPSc instead resists protease digestion. Prions carry no nucleic acid genome. Loss of membrane anchoring is not the converting step. Overtranscription of the PrP gene raises the amount of normal protein without changing its fold. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, prion protein isoforms); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: standard autoclaving with pressurized steam above 120°C may not reliably inactivate prions (First Aid Microbiology—Antimicrobials, disinfection and sterilization, p. 199). COMLEX: normal PrPc sits in the cytoplasm and on cell membranes, and its conversion is post-translational rather than genetic in sporadic disease (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which prion disorder affects deer and elk?","choices":{"A":"Feline spongiform encephalopathy","B":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy","C":"Mink transmissible encephalopathy","D":"Fatal familial insomnia","E":"Chronic wasting disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic wasting disease is the prion disease of deer and elk. Feline spongiform encephalopathy affects cats (general knowledge). Bovine spongiform encephalopathy affects cattle. Mink transmissible encephalopathy affects mink. Fatal familial insomnia is an inherited human prion disorder. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, animal prion diseases); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bovine spongiform encephalopathy is also called mad cow disease (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: prion diseases, also termed transmissible spongiform encephalopathies, arise sporadically, through inheritance, or by transmission (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Kuru plaques, the extracellular aggregates of abnormal prion protein, stain positively with which pair of stains?","choices":{"A":"Prussian blue and Giemsa","B":"Gram and acid-fast","C":"Oil red O and Sudan","D":"Congo red and PAS","E":"Trichrome and reticulin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kuru plaques are rounded extracellular PrPSc aggregates that are Congo red–positive and PAS-positive. Prussian blue marks iron, and Giemsa is a blood and parasite stain. Gram and acid-fast stains identify bacteria. Oil red O and Sudan stain neutral lipid. Trichrome and reticulin highlight collagen and reticular fibers. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, kuru plaques); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amyloid stained with Congo red looks red-orange under ordinary light and shows apple-green birefringence under polarized light (First Aid Pathology—Cellular injury, amyloidosis, p. 208). COMLEX: in advanced prion disease, severe neuronal loss and gliosis accompany coalescing vacuoles, a pattern called status spongiosus (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme cuts amyloid precursor protein inside the amyloid-beta sequence, so that no amyloid-beta peptide forms?","choices":{"A":"Beta-secretase","B":"Alpha-secretase","C":"Neprilysin","D":"Cathepsin D","E":"Insulin-degrading enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alpha-secretase cleaves cell-surface APP within the Aβ domain, releasing a benign soluble fragment in the nonamyloidogenic pathway. Beta-secretase, BACE1, starts the amyloidogenic pathway on endocytosed APP. Neprilysin degrades Aβ peptides after they form rather than cutting APP (general knowledge). Cathepsin D is a lysosomal protease that is not one of the APP-processing secretases (general knowledge). Insulin-degrading enzyme breaks down peptides after they form rather than preventing Aβ generation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, APP processing); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: senile plaques in gray matter have an extracellular Aβ core derived from cleavage of amyloid precursor protein (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: the Aβ42 peptide released by the amyloidogenic pathway is hydrophobic and aggregates readily (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"In Alzheimer disease, which neuropathologic measure tracks most closely with the clinical severity of dementia?","choices":{"A":"Neuritic plaque count","B":"Diffuse plaque count","C":"Tau tangle burden","D":"Ventricular volume","E":"Vascular amyloid load"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The regional burden and spread of neurofibrillary tangles correlate with dementia severity far better than amyloid plaques do. Neuritic plaque counts correlate less closely with clinical severity. Diffuse plaques lack dystrophic neurites and also occur in the basal ganglia and cerebellum. Ventricular volume enlarges passively as parenchyma is lost. Vascular amyloid load relates to hemorrhage risk rather than to dementia severity. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, role of tau); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: advanced age is the strongest risk factor for Alzheimer disease, which leads all causes of late-life dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: tangles are insoluble, so they persist as extracellular ghost or tombstone tangles after the neuron dies (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide makes up most of the vascular amyloid in cerebral amyloid angiopathy?","choices":{"A":"Amyloid-beta 40","B":"Amyloid-beta 42","C":"Transthyretin","D":"Serum amyloid A","E":"Beta-2 microglobulin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cerebral amyloid angiopathy deposits are predominantly Aβ40, laid down in the media and adventitia of small and medium meningeal and cortical arteries. Aβ42 is the aggregation-prone peptide released by the amyloidogenic pathway that seeds plaques. Transthyretin forms senile systemic and hereditary amyloid (general knowledge). Serum amyloid A forms AA amyloid in chronic inflammation (general knowledge). Beta-2 microglobulin forms dialysis-related amyloid (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, cerebral amyloid angiopathy); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amyloid angiopathy causes recurrent lobar intraparenchymal hemorrhage in older adults (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intraparenchymal hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: neuritic plaques measure 20 to 200 µm and have a dense amyloid core ringed by a clear halo, dystrophic neurites, astrocytes, and microglia (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"In Pick disease, which part of the temporal lobe is relatively spared by the lobar atrophy?","choices":{"A":"Anterior superior temporal gyrus","B":"Temporal pole and adjacent cortex","C":"Anterior inferior temporal gyrus","D":"Posterior superior temporal gyrus","E":"Medial temporal lobe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pick disease produces severe, asymmetric, circumscribed frontal and temporal atrophy with relative sparing of the posterior superior temporal gyrus. The anterior superior temporal gyrus lies within the atrophic anterior temporal region. The temporal pole and the cortex next to it are part of the atrophic anterior temporal region. The anterior inferior temporal gyrus is also involved. The medial temporal lobe is not the spared region in Pick disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, Pick disease subtype); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ex vacuo ventriculomegaly mimics hydrocephalus on imaging but reflects tissue loss, with normal intracranial pressure and no normal pressure hydrocephalus triad (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, hydrocephalus mimics, p. 536). COMLEX: FTLD is the preferred pathologic name because it describes the lobar pattern of atrophy rather than the clinical syndrome (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary change is characteristic of the behavioral variant of frontotemporal dementia?","choices":{"A":"Food refusal","B":"Early satiety","C":"Binge eating","D":"Craving ice","E":"Meal skipping"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Behavioral variant FTD brings hyperorality, with dietary changes, carbohydrate cravings, and binge eating. Food refusal reflects reduced intake, as in depression or advanced dementia. Early satiety is a gastrointestinal symptom. Craving ice, called pagophagia, suggests iron deficiency (general knowledge). Meal skipping is reduced intake rather than hyperorality. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, behavioral variant FTD). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral amygdala lesions cause Klüver-Bucy syndrome, with disinhibition, hyperphagia, hypersexuality, and hyperorality, classically after HSV-1 encephalitis (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: stereotyped, compulsive, or repetitive behaviors are part of the behavioral variant as well (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":1,"stem":"How does the incidence of frontotemporal lobar degeneration compare with that of Alzheimer disease in patients younger than 65 years?","choices":{"A":"About equal","B":"About one-tenth","C":"About one-half","D":"About double","E":"About fivefold"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FTLD is one of the most common causes of early-onset dementia, and in patients younger than 65 its incidence is comparable to that of Alzheimer disease. One-tenth would make it rare before 65. One-half understates its share of early-onset dementia. Double overstates it. Fivefold would make it the dominant cause before 65, which it is not. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, demographic distribution); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: frontotemporal dementia, formerly called Pick disease, starts with personality and behavior change or with primary progressive aphasia and may bring movement disorders (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: up to 30% to 50% of patients have a positive family history, and the genetic forms are prominently autosomal dominant (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 4 months of rapidly worsening memory and confusion. Six months ago she worked full time and managed the household finances, and she has no family history of neurologic disease and no history of surgery or blood transfusion. Examination shows disorientation to date and place and a wide-based, unsteady gait. A brain biopsy specimen shows many small, round vacuoles of varying size in the neuropil and within cortical neurons, with neuronal loss and gliosis. Which immunohistochemical finding in this tissue would confirm the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphorylated tau tangles","B":"Alpha-synuclein inclusions","C":"Phosphorylated TDP-43 inclusions","D":"Protease-resistant prion protein","E":"Amyloid-beta plaque cores"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dementia progressing over months with spongiform vacuolation of the neuropil and perikaryon fits sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, and definitive diagnosis rests on immunohistochemical detection of proteinase K–resistant PrPSc in brain tissue. Hyperphosphorylated tau tangles are intracellular lesions of Alzheimer disease and the tauopathies. Alpha-synuclein inclusions define Lewy body disorders (general knowledge). Phosphorylated TDP-43 inclusions mark FTLD-TDP. Amyloid-beta plaque cores are the extracellular lesion of Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, characteristic findings and definitive diagnosis); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, the EEG shows periodic sharp wave complexes and the CSF contains 14-3-3 protein (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: in sporadic CJD, PrPc converts spontaneously at a low rate, and the disease peaks in the seventh decade (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and misfolded protein aggregates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"In 1999, a 26-year-old man who has always lived in the United Kingdom is brought to the physician by his parents because of 9 months of low mood, anxiety, and withdrawal from friends, followed by increasing unsteadiness. He has no family history of neurologic disease. Examination shows an ataxic gait, and he recalls 0 of 3 words after 5 minutes. A brain biopsy specimen shows numerous extracellular plaques of abnormal prion protein throughout the cerebral cortex, each ringed by vacuolated neuropil. Which exposure most likely caused his disease?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated beef products","B":"Cadaveric growth hormone","C":"Dural graft from a cadaver","D":"Ritual funerary cannibalism","E":"Corneal transplant tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A young adult with early psychiatric symptoms and abundant cortical prion plaques encircled by spongiform change has variant CJD, which is linked to bovine spongiform encephalopathy acquired by eating contaminated bovine products. Cadaveric growth hormone, dural grafts, and corneal transplants transmit iatrogenic CJD, which does not typically produce this picture of a young adult with early psychiatric symptoms and florid cortical plaques, whereas he lived in the United Kingdom during the years of bovine spongiform encephalopathy exposure. Ritual cannibalism transmitted kuru in Papua New Guinea. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, variant CJD and kuru plaques); Robbins Ch. 28 (Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CJD is typically sporadic but can be transmitted by contaminated materials such as corneal transplants and neurosurgical equipment (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: kuru plaques cluster in the cerebellum in classic prion disease but spread widely through the cerebral cortex in vCJD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 months of rapidly progressive forgetfulness and difficulty walking. At age 9, he received a course of growth hormone injections extracted from human cadaveric pituitary glands for short stature. Examination shows impaired short-term recall and truncal ataxia. A brain biopsy specimen shows spongiform change in the cerebral cortex and deep gray matter with neuronal loss. Which process accounts for the spread of disease within his brain after the original exposure?","choices":{"A":"Viral replication in neurons","B":"Templated refolding of host PrP","C":"Autoantibodies against neurons","D":"Integration of prion DNA into host","E":"Microglial attack on myelin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inoculated PrPSc physically binds native PrPc and catalyzes its refolding into more PrPSc in a cooperative cascade, and the resulting aggregates spread from cell to cell; contaminated cadaveric growth hormone is a recognized source of iatrogenic CJD. Viral replication is not the mechanism because prions are not viruses. Autoantibodies against neuronal antigens cause autoimmune encephalitis rather than prion spread (general knowledge). Prions carry no DNA, so nothing integrates into the host genome. Microglial attack on myelin describes demyelinating disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, self-propagating conformational cascade; iatrogenic CJD); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kuru is an acquired prion disease described in tribal populations that practiced cannibalism (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: deep brain electrodes and dural or corneal grafts are other documented iatrogenic routes for CJD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — loss of proteostasis among the hallmarks of aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 3 years of progressive forgetfulness; he now misses work meetings and cannot manage the family finances. His father and paternal grandmother developed dementia in their mid-40s, and a paternal aunt was diagnosed with the same condition at age 48. Examination shows impaired delayed recall and difficulty naming uncommon objects, with normal strength and gait. MRI of the brain shows bilateral hippocampal and entorhinal atrophy, and genetic testing identifies a missense mutation in a gene on chromosome 14. Increased activity of which enzyme complex most likely contributes to his disease?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-secretase","B":"Beta-secretase","C":"Proteasome","D":"Acetylcholinesterase","E":"Gamma-secretase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autosomal dominant early-onset Alzheimer disease with a chromosome 14 mutation indicates PSEN1, whose gain-of-function mutations enhance gamma-secretase generation of Aβ42, since presenilin is part of that intramembranous complex. Alpha-secretase cuts within the Aβ domain and prevents Aβ formation. Beta-secretase begins amyloidogenic cleavage but is not the enzyme presenilin mutations enhance. The proteasome degrades ubiquitinated proteins and is not altered by this mutation (general knowledge). Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes acetylcholine and is not increased by presenilin mutations (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, early-onset familial AD; APP processing); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mutations in APP, presenilin-1, and presenilin-2 cause the familial forms of Alzheimer disease, about 10% of cases, which begin at a younger age (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: PSEN2 on chromosome 1 and point mutations in APP on chromosome 21 are the other early-onset familial causes (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with Down syndrome is brought to the physician by his sister because of 18 months of progressive forgetfulness; he can no longer follow his bus route to work or prepare the simple meals he once made. He had an atrioventricular septal defect repaired in infancy and takes no medications. Examination shows flat facies, upslanting palpebral fissures, and a single palmar crease, and he cannot recall any of 3 words after 5 minutes, which is below his baseline. His serum TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL), and his vitamin B12 is 520 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains the early onset of his dementia?","choices":{"A":"APOE epsilon-4 inheritance","B":"Extra APP gene dosage","C":"Presenilin-1 gain of function","D":"Prion protein misfolding","E":"Vascular amyloid deposition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chromosome 21 carries the APP gene, so trisomy 21 produces a gene-dosage excess of APP, and people with Down syndrome develop Alzheimer neuropathology by their second or third decade; his normal TSH and B12 argue against reversible causes. APOE ε4 lies on chromosome 19 and raises late-onset risk, which his karyotype does not explain. Presenilin-1 gain-of-function mutations on chromosome 14 cause autosomal dominant familial disease. Prion protein misfolding causes prion disease. Vascular amyloid deposition accompanies Alzheimer disease but is not the cause of early onset here. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, genetic determinants of Aβ accumulation); Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Pathogenesis). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95% of trisomy 21 cases arise from meiotic nondisjunction, most often in meiosis I, and the risk rises with maternal age (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, Down syndrome, p. 61). COMLEX: the rare APP A673T variant lowers Aβ generation and protects against cognitive decline (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics vs lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman dies in a nursing home after a 10-year course of progressive memory loss that ended with mutism and inability to walk. She had hypertension treated with lisinopril and no history of stroke. Neurologic examination in her final year showed no focal motor deficits. At autopsy, the brain weighs 980 g (reference range: 1200–1400 g) with narrowed gyri, widened sulci, and enlarged lateral ventricles, and assays of cortical tissue show choline acetyltransferase activity at 35% of age-matched control values. Loss of neurons in which structure most likely accounts for the enzyme finding?","choices":{"A":"Locus ceruleus","B":"Substantia nigra pars compacta","C":"Basal nucleus of Meynert","D":"Dorsal raphe nucleus","E":"Ventral tegmental area"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her course and atrophic brain indicate Alzheimer disease, in which marked loss of cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain nucleus basalis of Meynert severely depletes cortical choline acetyltransferase. The locus ceruleus is the main source of norepinephrine. The substantia nigra pars compacta supplies dopamine to the striatum. The dorsal raphe nucleus supplies serotonin. The ventral tegmental area supplies mesolimbic and mesocortical dopamine. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, cholinergic deficits and synaptic loss); First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, neurotransmitter changes with disease, p. 505. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: donepezil, rivastigmine, and galantamine are acetylcholinesterase inhibitors used first line, and memantine is an NMDA receptor antagonist for moderate to advanced disease (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, neurodegenerative disease therapy, p. 564). COMLEX: synaptic loss in Alzheimer disease shows up as reduced synaptophysin immunoreactivity (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of Parkinson disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 2 years of personality change; she makes crude jokes to strangers, has stopped bathing, and was recently caught shoplifting. Her mother had a similar illness that began at age 55. Examination shows apathy and perseveration, with intact orientation and recall of 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. MRI of the brain shows severe asymmetric atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes; she dies 6 years later, and at autopsy, silver staining of the frontal cortex shows swollen, ballooned neurons and smooth, round cytoplasmic inclusions. A germline mutation in which gene would most likely be found in this family?","choices":{"A":"GRN","B":"TARDBP","C":"C9orf72","D":"MAPT","E":"PSEN1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Early behavioral change with spared memory, frontotemporal atrophy, ballooned Pick cells, and silver-positive round Pick bodies indicate Pick disease, an FTLD-tau subtype. Pick disease is usually sporadic, but with an affected mother and an autosomal dominant pattern the likely cause in this family is familial FTLD-tau (MAPT), from germline mutations in MAPT on chromosome 17. GRN loss-of-function mutations produce FTLD-TDP with intranuclear TDP-43 inclusions. TARDBP mutations alter TDP-43 itself and also produce FTLD-TDP. C9orf72 expansions produce TDP-43 pathology rather than tau-positive Pick bodies. PSEN1 mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-tau and Pick disease subtype); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-tau). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inclusions in frontotemporal dementia contain either hyperphosphorylated tau, as round Pick bodies, or ubiquitinated TDP-43 (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: MAPT mutations can upset the ratio of tau splice isoforms or alter phosphorylation, so tau binds microtubules less and aggregates (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — atypical parkinsonian syndromes and how they differ from PD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 18 months of increasingly effortful, halting speech; she now speaks in short phrases with frequent speech sound errors but follows complex commands and remembers recent events. Her father and a paternal uncle had a similar progressive language disorder that began in their early 60s. Examination shows agrammatic speech with intact comprehension and normal strength, tone, and reflexes. MRI of the brain shows asymmetric left frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, and her father's autopsy report described needle-shaped intranuclear neuronal inclusions that stained for phosphorylated TDP-43. Which mechanism most likely underlies the disease in this family?","choices":{"A":"Tau splice isoform imbalance","B":"Amyloid-beta 42 overproduction","C":"Progranulin haploinsufficiency","D":"Alpha-synuclein aggregation","E":"FUS protein aggregation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Familial FTLD-TDP with needle-like, cat-eye intranuclear TDP-43 inclusions points to loss-of-function GRN mutations, which leave progranulin haploinsufficient and drive neuroinflammation; her effortful agrammatic speech is the nonfluent primary progressive aphasia presentation. Tau splice isoform imbalance underlies MAPT-related FTLD-tau, which lacks TDP-43 inclusions. Amyloid-beta 42 overproduction drives Alzheimer disease. Alpha-synuclein aggregation forms Lewy bodies (general knowledge). FUS protein aggregation defines the rarer FTLD-FUS subtype, whose inclusions lack TDP-43. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-TDP genetic etiologies, including the needle-like, cat-eye inclusions; language variants); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Broca aphasia from an inferior frontal gyrus lesion gives nonfluent speech with intact comprehension and preserved insight (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, aphasia, p. 529). COMLEX: TDP-43 is an RNA-binding protein normally held in the nucleus that mislocalizes in FTLD-TDP (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — contribution of inflammation to the aging process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 2 years of disinhibition and apathy; he makes inappropriate remarks to coworkers, has lost interest in his grandchildren, and shows no concern about these changes. Over the past 4 months, he has also had trouble climbing stairs and buttoning shirts; his mother had frontotemporal dementia, and a maternal uncle died of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis at age 60. Examination shows atrophy and fasciculations of the hand muscles, spasticity and hyperreflexia in the legs, and intact sensation, and he recalls 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. Electromyography shows active denervation and fasciculation potentials in the arms, legs, and thoracic paraspinal muscles. Which genetic abnormality is most likely present in this family?","choices":{"A":"C9orf72 repeat expansion","B":"MAPT missense mutation","C":"GRN loss of function","D":"HTT CAG repeat expansion","E":"PSEN1 missense mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A GGGGCC hexanucleotide expansion in the noncoding region of C9orf72 is the most common genetic cause of FTLD-TDP and joins FTLD and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis on one disease spectrum, matching his behavioral variant FTD with upper and lower motor neuron signs in a family with both disorders. MAPT missense mutations cause FTLD-tau. GRN loss of function causes FTLD-TDP with progranulin deficiency but is not the change that the notes tie to the FTLD and ALS spectrum. HTT CAG repeat expansion causes Huntington disease, with chorea rather than motor neuron disease (general knowledge). PSEN1 missense mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, C9orf72 expansion; disease progression and motor overlap); Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prefrontal lesions produce a frontal lobe syndrome of disinhibition, hyperphagia, impulsivity, loss of empathy, and impaired executive function, as seen in frontotemporal dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: FTLD-FUS is a rarer subtype marked by cytoplasmic aggregates of the RNA-binding protein FUS (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man has 10 months of worsening insomnia, followed by poor balance and forgetfulness. His father and a paternal aunt died of a similar illness in their 50s. Which result on genetic testing is expected?","choices":{"A":"PSEN1 missense mutation","B":"SOD1 point mutation","C":"Germline PRNP mutation","D":"HTT CAG repeat expansion","E":"APOE epsilon-4 homozygosity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progressive insomnia, then ataxia and cognitive decline, in a family with affected relatives in two generations fits fatal familial insomnia, an inherited prion disorder caused by specific germline PRNP mutations that speed the spontaneous conversion of PrPc. PSEN1 missense mutations cause early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. SOD1 point mutations cause some cases of familial ALS. An HTT CAG repeat expansion causes Huntington disease (general knowledge). APOE ε4 homozygosity raises the risk of late-onset Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, fatal familial insomnia; self-propagating conformational cascade). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a rapidly progressive dementia that is usually sporadic, with some familial forms (First Aid Microbiology—Systems, prions, p. 174). COMLEX: Gerstmann-Sträussler-Scheinker syndrome is the other inherited prion disease tied to germline PRNP mutations (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman dies 5 months after the onset of rapidly progressive dementia and ataxia. At autopsy, the cerebral cortex and deep gray matter show widespread vacuoles in the neuropil with neuronal loss and reactive gliosis. Which additional histologic finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Perivascular lymphocyte cuffs","B":"Neutrophil microabscesses","C":"Viral inclusion bodies","D":"Noncaseating granulomas","E":"Absent inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spongiform neurodegeneration in prion disease proceeds with a notable lack of inflammatory infiltrates. Perivascular lymphocyte cuffs are a feature of viral encephalitis (general knowledge). Neutrophil microabscesses point to bacterial infection (general knowledge). Viral inclusion bodies mark viral infection, and prions contain no genome. Noncaseating granulomas suggest sarcoidosis (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, absence of inflammation); Robbins Ch. 28 (Prion Diseases, Morphology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cortex in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is spongiform, with vacuolation but no inflammation (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: electron microscopy places the prion vacuoles inside membranes within the cytoplasm of neuronal processes (Robbins Ch. 28, Prion Diseases).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 7 — contribution of inflammation to the aging process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man whose mother developed Alzheimer disease at age 79 learns that he carries 2 copies of a risk allele of a lipid-transport gene on chromosome 19. Besides raising his overall risk, how does this genotype alter the disease if it develops?","choices":{"A":"Earlier age of onset","B":"Slower cognitive decline","C":"Lower amyloid burden","D":"Less tau pathology","E":"Sparing of the hippocampus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The APOE ε4 allele on chromosome 19 raises Alzheimer risk and lowers the age at which the disease begins. A slower cognitive decline is not an effect of this allele. Amyloid burden rises rather than falls, because ε4 enhances Aβ deposition. Tau pathology worsens with ε4 instead of lessening. The hippocampus is among the earliest and most severely atrophied structures in Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, late-onset sporadic AD risk; gross neuropathology). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ApoE-2 lowers and ApoE-4 raises the risk of sporadic Alzheimer disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: the ε4 allele accounts for about one-quarter of late-onset risk and also impairs lysosomal degradation and speeds neuroinflammation (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with early Alzheimer disease enrolls in a trial of an antibody meant to clear the amyloid-beta form that most directly causes synaptic failure and impairs long-term potentiation. Which form does the antibody target?","choices":{"A":"Dense-core plaque fibrils","B":"Soluble Aβ42 oligomers","C":"Vascular amyloid deposits","D":"Diffuse plaque deposits","E":"Paired helical filaments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Soluble Aβ42 oligomers are the primary neurotoxic species; they cause synaptic failure, disrupt long-term potentiation, activate the tyrosine kinase Fyn, and trigger downstream tau pathology. Dense-core plaque fibrils are insoluble deposits and not the leading synaptotoxic form. Vascular amyloid deposits make up cerebral amyloid angiopathy. Diffuse plaque deposits lack surrounding dystrophic neurites. Paired helical filaments are tau aggregates rather than amyloid-beta. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2, toxic oligomers). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurofibrillary tangles are intracellular aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau, and their number correlates with the degree of dementia (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: aggregated Aβ and tau activate microglia through the NLRP3 inflammasome, and microglia then reactivate complement-mediated pruning that destroys synapses (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 8 — accumulation of damaged macromolecules in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has 2 years of trouble with words. He speaks fluently but asks what \"giraffe\" and \"hammer\" mean, cannot name common objects, and misunderstands single words, while his memory for daily events is intact. Which disorder best accounts for his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Behavioral variant FTD","B":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","C":"Corticobasal degeneration","D":"Semantic dementia","E":"Nonfluent aphasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of word meaning with impaired naming and single-word comprehension, in fluent speech, is semantic dementia, a language variant of FTD. Behavioral variant FTD begins with personality and conduct changes. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a tauopathy whose overlap with FTD is extrapyramidal motor disease. Corticobasal degeneration is likewise an overlapping tauopathy with parkinsonian features. Nonfluent aphasia brings effortful, halting speech with speech sound errors rather than lost word meaning. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, language variants; disease progression and motor overlap). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wernicke aphasia from a superior temporal gyrus lesion gives fluent speech with impaired comprehension and poor insight (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, aphasia, p. 529). COMLEX: over several years the focal deficits of FTD progress to global cognitive impairment, mutism, and severe dependence (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — atypical parkinsonian syndromes and how they differ from PD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman's husband reports 1 year of new impulsivity, loss of empathy, and neglect of hygiene. Compared with early Alzheimer disease, which cognitive domain is expected to be relatively preserved early in her illness?","choices":{"A":"Episodic memory","B":"Executive judgment","C":"Social conduct","D":"Personality","E":"Behavioral inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"FTLD begins with early, striking changes in personality, social conduct, judgment, and language, while episodic memory stays relatively intact early, the reverse of Alzheimer disease. Executive judgment is impaired early in FTLD. Social conduct deteriorates early. Personality changes are a defining early feature. Behavioral inhibition is lost early, producing disinhibition. Sources: SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, distinct pattern of onset). Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: reversible causes of dementia include depression, hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, neurosyphilis, and normal pressure hydrocephalus (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 534). COMLEX: visuospatial orientation is also relatively preserved early in FTLD (SDL 6 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define the disorders classfied as Prion Diseases as well as their basic pathogenesis","batch":5,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her husband because of 2 months of rapidly worsening confusion, unsteady gait, and blurred, distorted vision. Examination shows disorientation and a wide-based ataxic gait. An EEG shows periodic sharp wave complexes. Which additional examination finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Resting tremor","B":"Startle myoclonus","C":"Fasciculations","D":"Choreiform movements","E":"Bilateral ptosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease begins with subtle memory and behavior changes and progresses to rapid dementia, often with prominent jerking contractions provoked by sudden stimuli, called startle myoclonus. A resting tremor suggests Parkinson disease (general knowledge). Fasciculations are lower motor neuron signs, as in ALS (general knowledge). Choreiform movements suggest Huntington disease (general knowledge). Bilateral ptosis points to a neuromuscular junction disorder such as myasthenia gravis (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease; the motor sign is not in the SDL 6 notes and also appears in First Aid); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 1, CJD); First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535 (EEG finding). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vascular dementia, the second most common dementia in older adults, follows multiple arterial infarcts or chronic ischemia and declines in a stepwise way (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: sporadic CJD has an annual incidence near 1 per million, makes up more than 90% of CJD, and kills in an average of about 7 months from symptom onset (Robbins Ch. 28, Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proposed pathogenesis as well as the pathologic findings in those affected by Alzheimer Disease","batch":5,"stem":"The family of an 81-year-old woman meets with the hospice team at her nursing home. She has had 9 years of progressive memory loss that has advanced to mutism, urinary incontinence, and inability to walk. Her daughter asks how the illness usually ends. Which event most commonly ends the course of this disease?","choices":{"A":"Intercurrent pneumonia","B":"Recurrent lobar hemorrhage","C":"Status epilepticus","D":"Pulmonary embolism","E":"Myocardial infarction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alzheimer disease runs a slow course, often longer than 10 years, ending with mutism, incontinence, and inability to walk, and an intercurrent illness, often pneumonia, is the usual terminal event. Recurrent lobar hemorrhage can complicate cerebral amyloid angiopathy but is not the usual terminal event. Status epilepticus is not the typical end of the disease (general knowledge). Pulmonary embolism is a risk of immobility but is not the usual cause of death in Alzheimer disease (general knowledge). Myocardial infarction is not the characteristic terminal event (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Alzheimer Disease, Clinical Features; the terminal event is Robbins-only); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 2). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lewy body dementia brings visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition and alertness, REM sleep behavior disorder, and parkinsonism (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: Alzheimer prevalence roughly doubles every 5 years, from about 1% at ages 60 to 64 to 40% or more at ages 85 to 89 (Robbins Ch. 28, Alzheimer Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — pathophysiology of infections in the elderly, including respiratory infection","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Discuss the features of frontotemporal lobar dementia including demographic distribution of involvement, pathologic findings, and clinical manifestations","batch":5,"stem":"A 60-year-old man is brought to the physician by his daughter because of 3 years of apathy, compulsive hoarding, and loss of empathy. His mother had a similar illness, and her autopsy showed phosphorylated, ubiquitinated inclusions of an RNA-binding protein in the cytoplasm of frontal cortical neurons, with loss of its normal nuclear staining. Examination shows perseveration and impaired verbal fluency, with intact orientation and recall. MRI of the brain shows bilateral frontal and anterior temporal atrophy. Besides RNA processing, the normal form of the protein in his mother's inclusions helps assemble which structures?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear pore complexes","B":"Autophagosomes","C":"Proteasome complexes","D":"Synaptic vesicles","E":"Stress granules"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The inclusions are TDP-43, whose normal roles include RNA processing and building stress granules, RNA-protein aggregates that hold free mRNAs when translation stops during cellular stress. Nuclear pore complexes are built from nucleoporins (general knowledge). Autophagosomes are double-membrane vesicles of the autophagy pathway (general knowledge). Proteasome complexes degrade ubiquitinated proteins (general knowledge). Synaptic vesicles store neurotransmitter at nerve terminals (general knowledge). Sources: Robbins Ch. 28 (Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations, FTLD-TDP; the stress-granule role is Robbins-only); SDL 6 lecture notes (Obj 3, FTLD-TDP). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 6 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 6 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS combines upper and lower motor neuron degeneration, and riluzole and edaravone slow functional decline (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, ALS, p. 544). COMLEX: unlike C9orf72 and TARDBP, GRN mutations have not been linked to ALS (Robbins Ch. 28, Frontotemporal Lobar Degenerations).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 7 — features of ALS","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"}],"7":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"On electron microscopy, the Lewy bodies of Parkinson disease consist of densely packed filaments of which type?","choices":{"A":"Intermediate filaments","B":"Paired helical filaments","C":"Actin microfilaments","D":"Hollow microtubules","E":"Collagen fibrils"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ultrastructurally, Lewy bodies are densely packed intermediate filaments built from aggregated alpha-synuclein. Paired helical filaments make up the tau tangles of Alzheimer disease. Actin microfilaments are not the Lewy body scaffold. Hollow microtubules are cytoskeletal tubes, not Lewy body filaments. Collagen fibrils are extracellular matrix proteins. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, Lewy bodies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oligodendrocytes, the predominant glial cell of white matter, each myelinate about 30 CNS axons and look like fried eggs on histology (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, CNS glial cells, p. 503). COMLEX: on light microscopy a Lewy body is a round to elongated eosinophilic inclusion with a dense core and a pale halo (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Epidemiologic studies link which exposures to a lower risk of Parkinson disease?","choices":{"A":"Pesticides and herbicides","B":"Solvents and jet fuel","C":"Organic vapors and MPTP","D":"Caffeine and nicotine","E":"Statins and aspirin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Caffeine and nicotine use are epidemiologically protective against Parkinson disease. Pesticides and herbicides are agricultural risk exposures. Industrial solvents and jet fuel also raise risk. Organic vapors and MPTP are neurotoxic exposures that raise risk. Statins and aspirin are not the protective exposures named for Parkinson disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, environmental risk factors). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine is made in the ventral tegmentum and the substantia nigra pars compacta of the midbrain and falls in Parkinson disease (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, neurotransmitter changes with disease, p. 505). COMLEX: agricultural chemicals, industrial solvents, organic vapors, and jet fuel are the environmental exposures tied to higher risk (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Heterozygous mutations in which gene, which encodes a lysosomal enzyme, are a major genetic risk factor for Parkinson disease?","choices":{"A":"LRRK2","B":"SNCA","C":"GBA1","D":"PINK1","E":"HEXA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Heterozygous GBA1 mutations reduce glucocerebrosidase activity, disrupt the autophagy-lysosome network, and account for about 5% of Parkinson disease. LRRK2 encodes a kinase, not a lysosomal enzyme. SNCA encodes alpha-synuclein. PINK1 encodes a mitochondrial kinase. HEXA encodes hexosaminidase A, which is deficient in Tay-Sachs disease (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, lysosomal and autophagic impairment). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous glucocerebrosidase deficiency causes Gaucher disease, the most common lysosomal storage disease, with hepatosplenomegaly, bone crises, and crumpled tissue paper macrophages (First Aid Biochemistry—Metabolism, lysosomal storage diseases, p. 86). COMLEX: reduced glucocerebrosidase activity impairs alpha-synuclein clearance and turns on the unfolded protein response (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"The resting tremor of Parkinson disease typically has which frequency?","choices":{"A":"1–2 Hz","B":"8–12 Hz","C":"15–20 Hz","D":"4–6 Hz","E":"25–30 Hz"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The parkinsonian resting tremor is slow, 4 to 6 Hz, and starts asymmetrically in a distal arm with a pill-rolling motion. A 1 to 2 Hz rhythm is slower than this tremor. Frequencies of 8 to 12 Hz, 15 to 20 Hz, and 25 to 30 Hz are faster than the classic resting tremor. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, resting tremor). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: essential tremor is a high-frequency postural tremor that is often familial, eases with alcohol, and is treated with propranolol or primidone (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: the parkinsonian tremor fades during purposeful movement and stops during sleep (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"Parkinsonian rigidity is distinguished from spasticity by which property of the resistance to passive movement?","choices":{"A":"Velocity-dependent tone","B":"Clasp-knife spastic tone","C":"Velocity-independent tone","D":"Decreased flaccid tone","E":"Fatigable resistance to stretch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Parkinsonian rigidity is resistance to passive joint movement that does not depend on velocity, felt as lead-pipe resistance or as cogwheeling when tremor is superimposed. Velocity-dependent tone defines spasticity (general knowledge). Clasp-knife tone is the sudden give of upper motor neuron spasticity (general knowledge). Decreased flaccid tone is a lower motor neuron sign. Resistance that fatigues with stretch is not a feature of rigidity. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, rigidity). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper motor neuron injury raises tone and reflexes with a positive Babinski sign and clasp-knife spasticity, whereas lower motor neuron injury lowers them (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, upper vs lower motor neuron lesions, p. 543). COMLEX: bradykinesia combines slow initiation and execution of movement with less spontaneous motor activity (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":1,"stem":"The masked facies of Parkinson disease includes which eye finding?","choices":{"A":"Facial fasciculations","B":"Decreased blink rate","C":"Bilateral ptosis","D":"Corneal reflex loss","E":"Lower facial droop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypomimia in Parkinson disease brings less facial animation, widened palpebral fissures, and a decreased blink rate. Facial fasciculations are lower motor neuron signs. Bilateral ptosis narrows rather than widens the fissures. Corneal reflex loss reflects trigeminal or facial nerve injury (general knowledge). Lower facial droop suggests an upper motor neuron facial lesion such as stroke (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, masked facies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbidopa blocks peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, so more levodopa reaches the brain and peripheral nausea and vomiting lessen (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: hypophonic dysarthria and micrographia accompany the loss of fine motor dexterity (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Under the one-year rule, dementia with Lewy bodies is diagnosed when dementia appears at what point relative to parkinsonism?","choices":{"A":"Within 1 year","B":"2–5 years later","C":"5–10 years later","D":"Over 10 years later","E":"At levodopa initiation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In dementia with Lewy bodies, dementia comes before, with, or within 1 year of the motor signs, reflecting an early limbic and neocortical synuclein burden. Onset 2 to 5 years later falls outside the rule. Onset 5 to 10 years later also falls well outside the 1-year window. Onset more than 10 years later is later still. Levodopa initiation is not the reference point. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: REM sleep behavior disorder, dream enactment from lost atonia, often precedes Parkinson disease and Lewy body dementia (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, sleep physiology, p. 507). COMLEX: Parkinson disease, Parkinson disease dementia, and dementia with Lewy bodies form one continuous spectrum of alpha-synucleinopathies (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Patients with dementia with Lewy bodies show profound hypersensitivity to which drug class?","choices":{"A":"Cholinesterase inhibitors","B":"Dopamine receptor agonists","C":"Benzodiazepine sedatives","D":"Beta-adrenergic blockers","E":"Typical antipsychotics"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is defined in part by profound hypersensitivity to traditional antipsychotic dopamine receptor antagonists. Cholinesterase inhibitors are not the drugs the hypersensitivity involves. Dopamine receptor agonists stimulate rather than block the receptor. Benzodiazepine sedatives act on GABA-A receptors. Beta-adrenergic blockers do not act on dopamine receptors. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, shared and distinguishing clinical features). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotics, typical more than atypical, with fever, encephalopathy, unstable vitals, raised CK, and lead-pipe rigidity, and it is treated with dantrolene and dopaminergic drugs (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, p. 587). COMLEX: fluctuating attention and alertness and early recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations also define the disorder (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":1,"stem":"Overall, about what share of patients with Parkinson disease develop dementia?","choices":{"A":"1–2%","B":"20–25%","C":"10–15%","D":"30–35%","E":"85–90%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Overall, dementia develops in about 10% to 15% of patients with Parkinson disease, a figure that rises to 40% to 80% only with long-term survival. A share of 1% to 2% is too low. Shares of 20% to 25% and 30% to 35% overstate the overall estimate and still fall short of the long-survival range. A share of 85% to 90% exceeds even the long-survival estimate. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, Parkinson disease dementia); Robbins Ch. 28 (Dementia With Lewy Bodies, share of PD patients with dementia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on-off fluctuations develop with progressive disease on levodopa, and the MAO-B inhibitors selegiline and rasagiline help smooth them (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: dementia with Lewy bodies brings early, recurrent, well-formed visual hallucinations (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Which clinical hallmark defines progressive supranuclear palsy?","choices":{"A":"Alien limb phenomenon","B":"Resting pill-rolling tremor","C":"Orthostatic hypotension","D":"Macular degeneration","E":"Impaired vertical saccades"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The hallmark of progressive supranuclear palsy is a supranuclear palsy of vertical gaze, with trouble starting voluntary downward or upward saccades. The alien limb phenomenon belongs to corticobasal degeneration. A resting pill-rolling tremor is typical of Parkinson disease and is absent in the atypical syndromes. Severe orthostatic hypotension points to autonomic failure in a different synucleinopathy. Macular degeneration with visual loss belongs to one of the spinocerebellar ataxias. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, progressive supranuclear palsy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: progressive supranuclear palsy is a Parkinson-plus syndrome with a hummingbird sign of midbrain atrophy on MRI (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, neurodegenerative movement disorders, p. 534). COMLEX: early backward falls, symmetric axial rigidity, pseudobulbar palsy, and a frontal subcortical dementia complete the picture (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions of alpha-synuclein within oligodendrocytes are the defining finding of which disorder?","choices":{"A":"Multiple system atrophy","B":"Progressive supranuclear palsy","C":"Corticobasal degeneration","D":"Dementia with Lewy bodies","E":"Parkinson disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiple system atrophy is a synucleinopathy defined by glial cytoplasmic inclusions of alpha-synuclein and ubiquitin in oligodendrocytes, with white matter tract degeneration. Progressive supranuclear palsy is a 4-repeat tauopathy. Corticobasal degeneration is also a 4-repeat tauopathy. Dementia with Lewy bodies has neuronal Lewy bodies. Parkinson disease has neuronal Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, multiple system atrophy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lesions of the subthalamic nucleus cause contralateral hemiballismus (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: as a group, the atypical syndromes progress quickly, begin symmetrically, impair gait early, and respond poorly or briefly to levodopa (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":1,"stem":"Progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration share which molecular classification?","choices":{"A":"Synucleinopathies","B":"4-repeat tauopathies","C":"TDP-43 proteinopathies","D":"Polyglutamine disorders","E":"Amyloid-beta disorders"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration are 4-repeat tauopathies classified under FTLD-tau. Synucleinopathies include Parkinson disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, and multiple system atrophy. TDP-43 proteinopathies include sporadic ALS and FTLD-TDP. Polyglutamine disorders include Huntington disease and several spinocerebellar ataxias. Amyloid-beta deposition defines the plaques of Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, molecular pathology of PSP and CBD). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal midbrain lesions cause Parinaud syndrome, often from a pineal gland tumor (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: corticobasal degeneration combines focal frontoparietal cortical atrophy with basal ganglia atrophy (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — frontotemporal lobar degeneration and FTLD-tau","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"What CAG repeat count in the huntingtin gene defines a pathogenic allele?","choices":{"A":"36 or more repeats","B":"20 or more repeats","C":"50 or more repeats","D":"100 or more repeats","E":"200 or more repeats"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal HTT alleles carry 6 to 35 CAG repeats, with 27 to 35 an intermediate range that can expand in offspring (GeneReviews, Huntington disease), and pathogenic alleles carry 36 or more. A threshold of 20 repeats falls inside the normal range. Thresholds of 50, 100, or 200 repeats are higher than the pathogenic cutoff. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, repeat thresholds). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in fragile X syndrome a CGG premutation of 50 to 200 repeats causes tremor, ataxia, and primary ovarian insufficiency, whereas more than 200 repeats is a full mutation (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: the HTT gene sits on chromosome 4p16.3 and encodes a 348-kDa protein (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"In Huntington disease, which striatal neurons are lost?","choices":{"A":"Noradrenergic ceruleus neurons","B":"Dopaminergic nigral neurons","C":"Glutamatergic pyramidal cells","D":"Medium spiny GABAergic neurons","E":"Serotonergic raphe neurons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Huntington disease destroys the medium-sized spiny GABAergic projection neurons that also express substance P, dynorphin, and enkephalin. Noradrenergic neurons lie in the locus ceruleus of the pons, not the striatum. Dopaminergic nigral neurons are lost in Parkinson disease. Glutamatergic pyramidal cells are cortical neurons. Serotonergic raphe neurons lie in the brainstem. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, selective neuronal loss). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the striatum is the putamen, which is motor, plus the caudate nucleus, which is cognitive, and the lentiform nucleus is the putamen plus the globus pallidus (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: surviving striatal and cortical neurons hold intranuclear ubiquitinated aggregates of huntingtin (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":1,"stem":"The expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene causes neuronal dysfunction through which genetic mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Haploinsufficiency","B":"Toxic gain of function","C":"Frameshift truncation","D":"Splice-site skipping","E":"Promoter hypermethylation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The elongated polyglutamine tract near the N-terminus of huntingtin confers a toxic gain of function that promotes abnormal protein conformation. Haploinsufficiency is loss of half the gene product and is not the HTT mechanism. Frameshift truncation is not produced by an in-frame CAG expansion. Splice-site skipping is not the mechanism of this exonic repeat. Promoter hypermethylation silences genes rather than adding a toxic protein. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, polyglutamine toxicity). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases can show anticipation, with greater severity and earlier onset in successive generations (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: transmission is autosomal dominant, with complete penetrance at 40 or more repeats and reduced penetrance at 36 to 39, and new sporadic mutations are very rare (SDL 7 notes; GeneReviews, Huntington disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"In Friedreich ataxia, the GAA repeat expansion lies in which part of the FXN gene?","choices":{"A":"First exon","B":"Promoter region","C":"Last exon","D":"Splice acceptor site","E":"First intron"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Friedreich ataxia is caused by an unstable GAA trinucleotide expansion within the first intron of FXN. The first exon is the location of the HTT CAG repeat, not the FXN repeat. The promoter region does not carry the expansion. The last exon does not carry it either. A splice acceptor site mutation is a different lesion type from a repeat expansion. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia molecular genetics). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myotonic dystrophy is an autosomal dominant CTG repeat disorder with cataracts, early balding in males, and gonadal atrophy (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, trinucleotide repeat expansion diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia is the most common hereditary ataxia and maps to chromosome 9q13 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"Which spinocerebellar ataxia is distinguished clinically by progressive macular degeneration that leads to blindness?","choices":{"A":"SCA7","B":"SCA1","C":"SCA2","D":"SCA3","E":"SCA6"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SCA7 is the polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxia set apart by progressive macular degeneration and visual loss. SCA1 is a polyglutamine ataxia without this retinal feature. SCA2 is likewise a polyglutamine ataxia without it. SCA3, also called Machado-Joseph disease, is not defined by macular degeneration. SCA6 is a polyglutamine ataxia without retinal degeneration. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, mechanistic subclassification of SCAs). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cerebellar hemisphere lesions cause intention tremor, limb ataxia, and loss of balance on the same side, with a fall toward the lesion (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: the SCAs are a group of more than 40 autosomal dominant disorders that usually start in early to mid-adulthood (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":1,"stem":"Frataxin is required to assemble which mitochondrial cofactors?","choices":{"A":"Copper-zinc centers","B":"Ubiquinone pools","C":"Iron-sulfur clusters","D":"Cristae junctions","E":"Mitochondrial ribosomes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Frataxin is essential for assembling mitochondrial iron-sulfur cluster complexes, so its loss disrupts complexes I and II, frees intramitochondrial iron, and generates reactive oxygen species. Copper-zinc centers are found in superoxide dismutase 1. Ubiquinone pools are not built by frataxin. Cristae junctions are membrane structures rather than frataxin products. Mitochondrial ribosomes are not assembled by frataxin. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia pathophysiologic defect). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondrial myopathies such as MELAS show ragged red fibers and vary in severity because of heteroplasmy (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, mitochondrial inheritance, p. 57; Biochemistry—Genetics, mitochondrial diseases, p. 60). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia also brings a high-arched pes cavus and progressive kyphoscoliosis (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — DNA damage and repair","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, Bunina bodies are found in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Cerebellar Purkinje cells","B":"Dorsal root ganglion cells","C":"Spinal oligodendrocytes","D":"Anterior horn cells","E":"Schwann cells of roots"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bunina bodies are small eosinophilic PAS-positive cytoplasmic inclusions in surviving anterior horn motor neurons and are thought to be remnants of autophagic vacuoles. Cerebellar Purkinje cells are not their site. Dorsal root ganglion cells are sensory neurons spared in ALS. Spinal oligodendrocytes are glia, not the neurons that carry Bunina bodies. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral roots and do not contain them. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, Bunina bodies). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: spinal muscular atrophy is autosomal recessive SMN1 loss with lower motor neuron signs only, giving a floppy baby with tongue fasciculations (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, spinal cord lesions, p. 544). COMLEX: anterior roots of the cord become thin and gray compared with the sensory roots, and the precentral gyrus atrophies (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"Which motor function is classically preserved until late in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Tongue protrusion strength","B":"Swallowing coordination","C":"Fine hand dexterity","D":"Diaphragmatic strength","E":"Extraocular eye movements"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Extraocular eye movements, along with sensation and sphincter control, are classically spared until late in ALS. Tongue strength falls with hypoglossal degeneration. Swallowing coordination fails with bulbar involvement. Fine hand dexterity is often the first function lost. Diaphragmatic strength declines as the disease advances. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, sparing). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in an adult, a Babinski sign, an upgoing big toe when the foot is stroked, may signify an upper motor neuron lesion (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, clinical reflexes, p. 523). COMLEX: about 80% to 90% of ALS is sporadic, and familial cases are mostly autosomal dominant (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":1,"stem":"Which ALS variant shows almost exclusively upper motor neuron signs?","choices":{"A":"Progressive muscular atrophy","B":"Primary lateral sclerosis","C":"Progressive bulbar palsy","D":"Spinal muscular atrophy","E":"Multifocal motor neuropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary lateral sclerosis is the variant marked by upper motor neuron signs with little lower motor neuron involvement. Progressive muscular atrophy is the uncommon variant in which lower motor neuron degeneration predominates. Progressive bulbar palsy begins with severe speech and swallowing impairment. Spinal muscular atrophy is an inherited lower motor neuron disease. Multifocal motor neuropathy is an immune-mediated neuropathy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, clinical variants). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: subacute combined degeneration from vitamin B12 deficiency demyelinates the dorsal columns along with the corticospinal and spinocerebellar tracts (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, spinal cord lesions, p. 544). COMLEX: progressive bulbar palsy evolves quickly and is often fatal within 1 to 2 years (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of 1 week of slowness, stiffness, and a shuffling gait. Two weeks ago he injected a street opioid synthesized in a home laboratory; he has no family history of movement disorders and takes no antipsychotic or antiemetic drugs. Examination shows a masked face, a 5-Hz resting tremor of both hands, cogwheel rigidity, and bradykinesia. Serum ceruloplasmin is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 20–40 mg/dL), and MRI of the brain shows no structural lesions. Damage to which neurons most likely explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Nigrostriatal dopamine neurons","B":"Striatal cholinergic interneurons","C":"Cerebellar Purkinje cells","D":"Anterior horn motor neurons","E":"Basal forebrain cholinergic neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acute parkinsonism after injecting a home-synthesized opioid points to MPTP, a mitochondrial neurotoxin that selectively damages nigrostriatal dopaminergic neurons; his normal ceruloplasmin argues against Wilson disease. Striatal cholinergic interneurons are not the MPTP target. Cerebellar Purkinje cell loss causes ataxia rather than parkinsonism (general knowledge). Anterior horn motor neuron loss causes weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations. Basal forebrain cholinergic neurons are lost in Alzheimer disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, environmental risk factors; core pathological definition). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wilson disease, an autosomal recessive ATP7B defect with low ceruloplasmin, deposits copper in the liver, basal ganglia, and cornea (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Pathology, Wilson disease, p. 402). COMLEX: at autopsy, the substantia nigra and locus ceruleus look pale because pigmented neuromelanin-containing catecholaminergic neurons have been lost (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of a left-hand tremor at rest and slowness when shaving. His father and an older sister were both diagnosed with Parkinson disease in their 60s. Examination shows a 4-Hz pill-rolling tremor of the left hand, cogwheel rigidity of the left wrist, and reduced arm swing on the left. Genetic testing identifies a heterozygous gain-of-function missense mutation in the kinase domain of a large leucine-rich repeat protein. The mutation most likely disrupts vesicular trafficking by hyperphosphorylating which proteins?","choices":{"A":"Tau proteins","B":"Histone proteins","C":"Neurofilaments","D":"Synaptotagmins","E":"Rab GTPases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autosomal dominant Parkinson disease with a kinase-domain mutation fits LRRK2, whose hyperactivated kinase hyperphosphorylates Rab GTPases and disturbs vesicular trafficking. Tau hyperphosphorylation drives the tauopathies. Histone phosphorylation is a chromatin modification and not the LRRK2 mechanism. Neurofilaments are cytoskeletal proteins not named as LRRK2 targets. Synaptotagmins are calcium sensors for vesicle release (general knowledge) rather than the LRRK2 substrates. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, LRRK2 kinase mutations). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus or internal globus pallidus helps advanced Parkinson disease, and non-ergot dopamine agonists are favored in younger patients (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563); the AAN 2021 early Parkinson disease guideline instead makes levodopa first-line and reserves dopamine agonists for select patients younger than 60 (Pringsheim et al., Neurology 2021). COMLEX: SNCA point mutations, duplications, or triplications promote toxic alpha-synuclein oligomers in other inherited forms (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 years of slowly worsening stiffness and a right-foot tremor. Her parents are first cousins and are unaffected, and her younger brother has a similar disorder. Examination shows rigidity and bradykinesia that are worse on the right, and her symptoms improve markedly with levodopa. Genetic testing shows biallelic loss-of-function mutations in PRKN, which encodes the E3 ubiquitin ligase Parkin. Accumulation of which of the following most directly drives neuronal injury in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Hyperphosphorylated tau","B":"Amyloid-beta oligomers","C":"Defective mitochondria","D":"Polyglutamine huntingtin","E":"Unrepaired DNA breaks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PINK1 normally recruits and activates Parkin to send damaged mitochondria for autophagic degradation, so recessive loss of Parkin lets dysfunctional mitochondria build up, generating reactive oxygen species and lowering complex I activity. Hyperphosphorylated tau accumulates in Alzheimer disease and the tauopathies. Amyloid-beta oligomers drive Alzheimer disease. Polyglutamine huntingtin accumulates in Huntington disease. Unrepaired DNA breaks accumulate in ataxia-telangiectasia. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 1, mitochondrial dysfunction and mitophagy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mitochondrial disorders follow maternal inheritance, with all children of an affected mother at risk and none of the children of an affected father (First Aid Biochemistry—Genetics, modes of inheritance, p. 57). COMLEX: PARK7, which encodes DJ-1, is the third recessive gene that points to mitochondrial quality control (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man with a 6-year history of Parkinson disease comes to the physician because of constipation with 2 bowel movements a week despite adequate fiber and fluid intake. He takes carbidopa-levodopa and no opioids or anticholinergic drugs. Examination shows a resting tremor, rigidity, and a soft, nontender abdomen with normal bowel sounds. His serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL), and his TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Lewy body pathology in which structure most likely explains his constipation?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal vagal motor nucleus","B":"Intermediolateral cell column","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Edinger-Westphal nucleus","E":"Inferior salivatory nucleus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autonomic dysfunction in Parkinson disease, including constipation, arises from Lewy body pathology in the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and in sympathetic ganglia; his normal calcium and TSH exclude metabolic causes. The intermediolateral cell column is a spinal cord column rather than the vagal parasympathetic output to the gut (general knowledge). The nucleus tractus solitarius is a visceral sensory nucleus (general knowledge). The Edinger-Westphal nucleus controls pupillary constriction and accommodation (general knowledge). The inferior salivatory nucleus supplies the parotid gland (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, autonomic dysfunction). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the antimuscarinics benztropine and trihexyphenidyl improve tremor and rigidity but do little for bradykinesia (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: orthostatic hypotension, bladder urgency, incontinence, and poor temperature regulation are the other autonomic features (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — gastrointestinal disease in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 1 year of a right-hand tremor at rest and slowness when dressing. She has no history of antipsychotic or antiemetic use, and her family history is negative for tremor. Examination shows a 5-Hz pill-rolling tremor of the right hand that disappears when she reaches for a cup, cogwheel rigidity at the right wrist, reduced right arm swing, and micrographia. Laboratory studies show a TSH of 2.0 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL) and a vitamin B12 of 610 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which response to a drug trial would most strongly support idiopathic Parkinson disease over an atypical parkinsonian syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Tremor relief with propranolol","B":"Sustained levodopa benefit","C":"Tremor relief with alcohol","D":"Improvement with donepezil","E":"Improvement with haloperidol"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A dramatic, sustained initial improvement after dopamine replacement with levodopa or a dopamine agonist supports idiopathic Parkinson disease, whereas the atypical syndromes respond poorly or briefly. Tremor relief with propranolol points to essential tremor. Tremor relief with alcohol is also typical of essential tremor. Donepezil is a cholinesterase inhibitor for dementia and does not test dopaminergic responsiveness (general knowledge). Haloperidol blocks dopamine receptors and worsens parkinsonism (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, therapeutic responsiveness; Obj 4, general distinctions). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: resting tremor, chorea, and athetosis localize to basal ganglia lesions such as those of Parkinson and Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: hypophonia makes the voice soft and monotonous in Parkinson disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man comes to the physician because of 18 months of slowness in walking and difficulty getting out of chairs. He has a mild, intermittent tremor of the left hand at rest and takes no dopamine-blocking drugs. Examination shows reduced facial expression, slow, small finger taps that decrement in amplitude, and mild rigidity of the left arm. Dopamine transporter SPECT shows striatal tracer uptake at 40% of age-matched normal values, worse on the right. Which cardinal feature of his disease tracks most closely with the degree of striatal dopamine depletion?","choices":{"A":"Resting tremor","B":"Postural instability","C":"Hyposmia","D":"Bradykinesia","E":"Constipation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bradykinesia, the slowness in starting and carrying out movement, is typically proportional to the degree of striatal dopamine depletion. Resting tremor is mild in this patient and does not track depletion as closely. Postural instability emerges later with disease progression. Hyposmia is a nonmotor feature rather than a cardinal motor sign (general knowledge). Constipation is an autonomic nonmotor feature. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, bradykinesia and akinesia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nigrostriatal dopamine stimulates the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors, both of which favor movement (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: postural instability brings backward falls and an abnormal response to the pull test as the disease advances (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the physician by his wife because of 8 months of episodes of confusion that alternate with near-normal days; he often says he sees children playing in the living room. Over the past 4 months, he has developed a shuffling gait and stiffness. Examination shows inattention on serial sevens, mild symmetric rigidity, and bradykinesia without tremor. His serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and MRI of the brain shows mild generalized atrophy without infarcts. At autopsy, Lewy bodies are most likely to be found early in which distribution?","choices":{"A":"Lower brainstem nuclei","B":"Widespread cerebral cortex","C":"Spinal anterior horns","D":"Cerebellar Purkinje cell layer","E":"Striatal oligodendroglia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cognitive decline that comes before parkinsonism, with fluctuating attention and well-formed visual hallucinations, fits dementia with Lewy bodies, in which Lewy bodies appear early throughout the frontal, temporal, and parietal cortices and cingulate gyrus. Lower brainstem nuclei are the early site in idiopathic Parkinson disease. Spinal anterior horns are the site of motor neuron loss in ALS. The cerebellar Purkinje cell layer is not the early site. Striatal oligodendroglial inclusions suggest multiple system atrophy. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, neuropathological concordance; the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lewy body dementia is called so when cognitive and motor symptoms begin less than 1 year apart, and otherwise the dementia is attributed to Parkinson disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, dementia, p. 535). COMLEX: both disorders share neuronal loss and depigmentation in the substantia nigra and locus ceruleus (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with a 14-year history of asymmetric Parkinson disease is brought to the physician because of 1 year of slowed thinking and difficulty managing his medications. His motor symptoms responded well to levodopa for many years. Examination shows bradykinesia, rigidity worse on the left, and slowed, inattentive responses with intact orientation. MRI of the brain shows mild generalized atrophy, and his serum vitamin B12 is 540 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which biological feature links his disease with dementia with Lewy bodies?","choices":{"A":"Glial synuclein inclusions","B":"Tau-positive tufted astrocytes","C":"Identical synuclein fold","D":"Polyglutamine aggregates","E":"TDP-43 inclusions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Parkinson disease dementia and dementia with Lewy bodies share genetic susceptibility loci and the identical alpha-synuclein molecular fold on structural analysis, making them phenotypes on one spectrum. Glial synuclein inclusions, with a distinct oligodendroglial fold, define multiple system atrophy. Tau-positive tufted astrocytes are not a synuclein fold. Polyglutamine aggregates occur in Huntington disease and several SCAs. TDP-43 inclusions characterize sporadic ALS and FTLD-TDP. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, unified clinicopathological spectrum). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: entacapone and tolcapone inhibit COMT to prevent peripheral breakdown of levodopa to 3-O-methyldopa, and tolcapone also acts centrally (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: in idiopathic Parkinson disease, Lewy pathology climbs from the medulla through the pons and midbrain before reaching limbic cortex and neocortex (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman dies in a nursing home after 5 years of dementia with marked day-to-day swings in alertness and recurrent visual hallucinations. Mild parkinsonism appeared 6 months after her memory problems began. Her final examination showed symmetric rigidity and bradykinesia without tremor. At autopsy, immunostaining shows numerous neuronal inclusions of phosphorylated alpha-synuclein. Which additional pathology is commonly present in varying degrees in this disorder?","choices":{"A":"Amyloid plaques and tangles","B":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","C":"Spongiform vacuolation","D":"Tufted tau astrocytes","E":"Intranuclear huntingtin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dementia with Lewy bodies is accompanied by varying degrees of coexisting Alzheimer pathology, with amyloid-beta plaques and tau tangles. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Spongiform vacuolation is the lesion of prion disease. Tufted tau astrocytes are a tauopathy lesion rather than coexisting Alzheimer pathology. Intranuclear huntingtin aggregates occur in Huntington disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, neuropathological concordance). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the non-ergot dopamine agonists pramipexole and ropinirole can cause impulse control disorders such as gambling, along with postural hypotension and hallucinations (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: SNCA, GBA1, and APOE epsilon-4 are shared susceptibility loci across the Lewy body spectrum (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because of 1 year of frequent backward falls and trouble reading and walking downstairs. A 6-week trial of levodopa produced no improvement, and he has no tremor. Examination shows marked symmetric rigidity of the neck and trunk, slowed vertical saccades with intact oculocephalic movements, and dysarthria with emotional lability. MRI of the brain shows midbrain atrophy with a preserved pons. Autopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Round Pick bodies","B":"Tufted astrocytes","C":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","D":"Cortical Lewy bodies","E":"Bunina bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early backward falls, axial rigidity, a supranuclear vertical gaze palsy, and levodopa resistance indicate progressive supranuclear palsy, a 4-repeat tauopathy with globular tau-positive tangles and tufted astrocytes in the subthalamic nucleus, globus pallidus, substantia nigra, and midbrain. Round Pick bodies are the tau inclusions of Pick disease. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Cortical Lewy bodies characterize dementia with Lewy bodies. Bunina bodies occur in ALS. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, progressive supranuclear palsy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amantadine increases dopamine release and blocks its reuptake, is used mainly against levodopa-induced dyskinesias, and can cause livedo reticularis and peripheral edema (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, Parkinson disease therapy, p. 563). COMLEX: the absence of a pill-rolling resting tremor and early prominent falls help separate the atypical syndromes from Parkinson disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 years of progressive stiffness and clumsiness of her right arm; she says the arm sometimes rises and grasps objects on its own. Levodopa produced no benefit. Examination shows severe rigidity and dystonic posturing of the right arm with myoclonic jerks, inability to mime using a comb with that hand, and impaired two-point discrimination on the right with normal primary sensation. MRI of the brain shows asymmetric left frontoparietal cortical atrophy. Which finding is expected in this patient's brain tissue?","choices":{"A":"Glial cytoplasmic inclusions","B":"Brainstem Lewy bodies","C":"Intranuclear huntingtin","D":"Astrocytic tau plaques","E":"Spongiform vacuolation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Strikingly asymmetric rigidity and dystonia with myoclonus, ideomotor apraxia, cortical sensory loss, and an alien limb indicate corticobasal degeneration, a 4-repeat tauopathy with tau-positive astrocytic plaques, coiled bodies in oligodendrocytes, and ballooned achromatic neurons. Glial cytoplasmic inclusions define multiple system atrophy. Brainstem Lewy bodies are the early lesion of Parkinson disease. Intranuclear huntingtin aggregates occur in Huntington disease. Spongiform vacuolation is the lesion of prion disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, corticobasal degeneration). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dystonia is a sustained involuntary muscle contraction, as in writer's cramp, blepharospasm, and torticollis, and it is treated with botulinum toxin injection (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: the alien limb makes involuntary movements that look purposeful but that the patient feels are beyond voluntary control (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — FTLD-tau pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man comes to the physician because of 18 months of progressive unsteadiness, light-headedness on standing, erectile dysfunction, and urinary incontinence. Levodopa has not helped. His blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg supine and 96/60 mm Hg after 3 minutes standing, with no compensatory rise in heart rate, and examination shows a wide-based ataxic gait, dysmetria, and reduced sweating. MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the pons, middle cerebellar peduncles, and cerebellum. Degeneration of which structure most directly accounts for his orthostatic hypotension?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Intermediolateral cell column","D":"Nucleus ambiguus neurons","E":"Lateral corticospinal tracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cerebellar ataxia with pontocerebellar atrophy, levodopa-refractory course, and severe autonomic failure fit multiple system atrophy, in which degeneration of the catecholaminergic medullary centers and the spinal intermediolateral cell columns causes orthostatic hypotension, anhidrosis, erectile dysfunction, and incontinence. Dorsal root ganglion sensory neurons carry afferent input rather than sympathetic output. Anterior horn motor neurons control skeletal muscle. Nucleus ambiguus neurons supply the pharynx, larynx, and cardiac vagal output rather than sympathetic vasoconstriction (general knowledge). Lateral corticospinal tracts are descending motor pathways rather than autonomic ones. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, multiple system atrophy). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intention tremor is a slow zigzag movement toward a target caused by cerebellar dysfunction (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: atypical parkinsonian syndromes progress rapidly from a symmetric onset and lack the classic pill-rolling resting tremor (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man is brought to the physician because of 1 year of irritability, impulsive outbursts, and brief involuntary jerking movements of his face and arms. His father developed similar symptoms at age 45, and his paternal grandfather at age 60. Examination shows irregular, dancelike movements of the face, trunk, and limbs and impaired rapid alternating movements. Genetic testing shows 58 CAG repeats in one HTT allele, compared with 44 repeats in his father. Which process best explains his earlier onset?","choices":{"A":"Maternal mitochondrial inheritance","B":"Germline mosaicism in mother","C":"Somatic repeat contraction","D":"Uniparental disomy of chromosome 4","E":"Expansion during spermatogenesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CAG tracts are meiotically unstable and tend to expand during paternal spermatogenesis, so paternal transmission yields longer repeats and earlier, more severe disease, and repeat length correlates inversely with age of onset. Maternal mitochondrial inheritance does not apply to a nuclear gene transmitted from the father. Germline mosaicism in his mother does not explain a paternally inherited expansion. Somatic contraction would shorten rather than lengthen the repeat. Uniparental disomy is not the mechanism of anticipation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, genetic anticipation). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chorea is sudden, jerky, purposeless movement from basal ganglia lesions, seen in Huntington disease and in Sydenham chorea of acute rheumatic fever (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: as striatal degeneration advances, chorea gives way to dystonia, bradykinesia, rigidity, and dysarthria (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q35","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman is brought to the physician by her husband because of 2 years of depression, poor planning, and fidgety movements she cannot control. Her mother died in her 50s of a similar illness. Examination shows continuous irregular movements of the face and fingers, motor impersistence of tongue protrusion, and slowed saccades. MRI of the brain shows atrophy of the caudate nuclei and putamen with box-shaped enlargement of the frontal horns of the lateral ventricles. Loss of the inhibitory output of her damaged striatal neurons most directly disinhibits which pathway, producing her movements?","choices":{"A":"Spinocerebellar pathway","B":"Dorsal column pathway","C":"Mesolimbic dopamine pathway","D":"Thalamocortical pathway","E":"Rubrospinal pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of striatal projection neurons in Huntington disease disinhibits the thalamocortical motor pathway, producing hyperkinetic chorea. The spinocerebellar pathway carries proprioceptive input to the cerebellum. The dorsal column pathway carries vibration and position sense. The mesolimbic dopamine pathway is involved in reward rather than chorea (general knowledge). The rubrospinal pathway is a brainstem motor tract not disinhibited by striatal loss (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, macroscopic striatal atrophy; selective neuronal loss). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the indirect pathway, striatal GABA inhibits the external globus pallidus, which disinhibits the subthalamic nucleus, which then drives the internal globus pallidus to inhibit the thalamus (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, basal ganglia, p. 511). COMLEX: emotional blunting, irritability, and anxiety are early personality changes in Huntington disease (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of FTLD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with a 15-year history of a hereditary movement disorder dies of aspiration pneumonia in a nursing home. In his final years he had severe dementia, rigidity, and dysarthria, after earlier years of chorea. His father and a paternal aunt had the same illness. At autopsy, the brain weighs 1050 g (reference range: 1200–1400 g) with marked caudate and putamen atrophy, and surviving striatal neurons contain ubiquitinated intranuclear aggregates. The aggregated protein contains an abnormally long tract of which amino acid?","choices":{"A":"Glutamine","B":"Glycine","C":"Alanine","D":"Glutamate","E":"Asparagine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The CAG expansion in exon 1 of HTT is translated into an elongated polyglutamine tract near the N-terminus of huntingtin. Glycine is not encoded by CAG. Alanine is not encoded by CAG either. Glutamate differs from glutamine and is not the expanded residue. Asparagine is not the amino acid encoded by the repeat. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, polyglutamine toxicity; disease course). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Huntington disease, dopamine rises while GABA and acetylcholine fall in the brain, and neurons die through NMDA receptor binding and glutamate excitotoxicity (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, neurodegenerative movement disorders, p. 534). COMLEX: average survival is about 15 years after symptom onset, and aspiration pneumonia is the most common terminal event (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — protein homeostasis and cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q37","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of 3 years of progressive clumsiness and frequent falls. His parents are unaffected, and an older sister has the same problem. Examination shows a wide-based ataxic gait, absent ankle reflexes with bilateral extensor plantar responses, loss of vibration and position sense in the feet, high-arched feet, and kyphoscoliosis. Echocardiography shows an interventricular septal thickness of 16 mm (reference range: 6–11 mm), and his fasting plasma glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). The mutation responsible for his disorder reduces gene expression by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Polyglutamine protein toxicity","B":"Nonsense-mediated mRNA decay","C":"Heterochromatin gene silencing","D":"Dominant-negative frataxin","E":"Toxic nuclear RNA foci"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autosomal recessive childhood ataxia with areflexia, extensor plantars, posterior column loss, pes cavus, scoliosis, and septal hypertrophy indicates Friedreich ataxia, in which the intronic GAA expansion promotes heterochromatin formation and silences FXN. Polyglutamine protein toxicity is the mechanism of Huntington disease and the polyglutamine SCAs. Nonsense-mediated decay follows premature stop codons rather than an intronic repeat (general knowledge). A dominant-negative protein does not fit recessive frataxin deficiency. Toxic RNA foci are a mechanism of noncoding expansions such as C9orf72. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, Friedreich ataxia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia degenerates the corticospinal tracts, spinocerebellar tracts, dorsal columns, and dorsal root ganglia, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the cause of death (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Friedreich ataxia, p. 545). COMLEX: Friedreich ataxia begins in the first or second decade with progressive gait ataxia and clumsiness (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — changes in the cardiovascular system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q38","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of an unsteady gait since she began walking and three episodes of pneumonia in the past 2 years. Her parents are unaffected first cousins. Examination shows truncal ataxia, writhing movements of the hands, and dilated tortuous vessels on the bulbar conjunctivae and ears. Her serum IgA is 10 mg/dL (reference range for age: 33–200 mg/dL). For which malignancy is she at greatest risk?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Neuroblastoma","C":"Hepatoblastoma","D":"Retinoblastoma","E":"T-cell lymphoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Childhood cerebellar ataxia with choreoathetosis, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, recurrent sinopulmonary infections, and low IgA indicate ataxia-telangiectasia, which carries a marked susceptibility to lymphoid neoplasms, particularly T-cell leukemias and lymphomas. Medulloblastoma is not the characteristic malignancy (general knowledge). Neuroblastoma is not linked to this syndrome (general knowledge). Hepatoblastoma is not linked to it either (general knowledge). Retinoblastoma arises from RB1 mutations (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, ataxia-telangiectasia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in ataxia-telangiectasia, defective ATM fails to detect DNA damage and halt the cell cycle, giving the triad of cerebellar defects, spider angiomas, and IgA deficiency (First Aid Immunology—Immune Responses, immunodeficiencies, p. 115). COMLEX: patients are hypersensitive to ionizing radiation, and the gene maps to chromosome 11q22-q23 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — pathophysiology and risk factors for infection in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q39","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man comes to the physician because of 3 years of worsening unsteadiness and slurred speech. His father and paternal grandfather developed the same problem in their 40s, and his two younger siblings are unaffected so far. Examination shows a wide-based gait, dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia, gaze-evoked nystagmus, and scanning dysarthria. Genetic testing shows an expanded CAG repeat in a spinocerebellar ataxia gene, and MRI of the brain shows cerebellar and pontine atrophy. What is the risk that each of his children will inherit the disorder?","choices":{"A":"25%","B":"75%","C":"67%","D":"50%","E":"10%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The spinocerebellar ataxias are inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so each child of an affected parent has a 50% risk. A 25% risk applies to children of two carriers of a recessive disorder. A 75% risk would require both parents to be affected heterozygotes, which his history does not suggest (general knowledge). A 67% figure is the carrier probability for an unaffected sibling in a recessive disorder (general knowledge). A 10% risk does not match any Mendelian pattern here. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, spinocerebellar ataxias). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cerebellum receives contralateral cortical input through the middle cerebellar peduncle and sends output through the deep nuclei and superior peduncle (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, cerebellum, p. 510). COMLEX: noncoding repeat expansions such as SCA10, SCA12, and SCA31 form a second mechanistic group of SCAs (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q40","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man dies 3 years after the onset of asymmetric weakness of his left hand. During his illness he developed atrophy and fasciculations of the hand and tongue muscles, brisk reflexes, and bilateral extensor plantar responses, with normal sensation and bladder control. Electromyography had shown active denervation in all four limbs, and nerve conduction studies showed normal sensory responses. At autopsy, the anterior spinal roots are thin and gray compared with the posterior roots. A myelin stain of the spinal cord would most likely show pallor in which region?","choices":{"A":"Lateral corticospinal tracts","B":"Posterior dorsal columns","C":"Spinocerebellar tracts","D":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","E":"Medial longitudinal fasciculi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Combined upper and lower motor neuron signs indicate ALS, in which loss of large myelinated axons in the lateral corticospinal tracts leaves them pale on myelin stains. The posterior dorsal columns carry sensory fibers spared in ALS. The spinocerebellar tracts degenerate in Friedreich ataxia. The anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the pale region. The medial longitudinal fasciculi coordinate eye movements, which stay intact. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, microscopic hallmarks). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALS gives lower motor neuron signs such as fasciculations and bulbar palsy with upper motor neuron signs such as spasticity, hyperreflexia, and pseudobulbar palsy, without sensory or bowel and bladder deficits (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, p. 544). COMLEX: anterior horn motor neurons are depleted from cervical to lumbosacral levels with extensive fibrillary astrogliosis (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q41","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with a 1-year history of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis is referred for cognitive testing because her husband says she has trouble planning meals and paying bills. She has progressive weakness of both legs and fasciculations but no hallucinations or personal history of stroke. Examination shows spastic paraparesis, hyperreflexia, and atrophy of the thigh muscles, and she recalls 3 of 3 words at 5 minutes. Her serum vitamin B12 is 480 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and her TSH is 2.2 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Testing is most likely to show deficits in which cognitive domain?","choices":{"A":"Visuospatial function","B":"Episodic memory","C":"Sensory perception","D":"Remote memory","E":"Executive function"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Up to half of patients with ALS have measurable frontotemporal cognitive or behavioral deficits, most often executive dysfunction, and about 15% meet criteria for frontotemporal dementia. Visuospatial function is not the domain usually affected. Episodic memory is intact on her recall testing. Sensory perception is spared in ALS. Remote memory is not the characteristic deficit. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, the ALS–frontotemporal spectrum). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prefrontal lesions give disinhibition, impulsivity, and impaired executive function as part of a frontal lobe syndrome (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, common brain lesions, p. 524). COMLEX: TDP-43 inclusions are present in sporadic, C9orf72, and TARDBP cases of ALS but absent in SOD1 and FUS cases (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — FTLD-TDP and the ALS spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q42","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 6 months of slurred speech, choking on liquids, and a hoarse voice. She has lost 5 kg, and her limbs feel normal. Examination shows a wasted tongue with visible fasciculations, weak palatal elevation, a brisk jaw jerk, and normal limb strength and sensation. Electromyography of the tongue shows active denervation, and MRI of the brain and cervical spine shows no structural lesion. Degeneration of which nucleus explains her tongue findings?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Facial motor nucleus","D":"Motor trigeminal nucleus","E":"Abducens nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bulbar ALS presents with dysarthria, dysphagia, hoarseness, and tongue fasciculations and atrophy from degeneration of motor cranial nerve nuclei; the tongue findings localize to the hypoglossal nucleus. The nucleus ambiguus explains her hoarseness and palatal weakness rather than the tongue. The facial motor nucleus supplies the muscles of facial expression (general knowledge). The motor trigeminal nucleus supplies the muscles of mastication. The abducens nucleus controls lateral gaze, which is spared. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, bulbar involvement). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a lower motor neuron lesion of CN XII makes the tongue deviate toward the side of the lesion (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, cranial nerve lesions, p. 546). COMLEX: autophagy receptor genes such as SQSTM1 and OPTN, and cytoskeletal genes such as TUBA4A and PFN1, also drive ALS (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — gastrointestinal disease in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the clinical findings in patients with Parkinson's Disease","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old man with Parkinson disease walks stooped with short steps; once moving, his steps involuntarily speed up and he has trouble stopping. Which term describes this finding?","choices":{"A":"Circumduction","B":"Steppage gait","C":"Festination","D":"Waddling gait","E":"Scissor gait"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Festination is an involuntary, uncontrollable forward acceleration of steps in the parkinsonian gait. Circumduction is the outward swing of a spastic leg after stroke (general knowledge). Steppage gait is high stepping from foot drop (general knowledge). Waddling gait reflects proximal hip weakness (general knowledge). Scissor gait is crossing of spastic legs (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 2, parkinsonian festinating gait). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: athetosis is a slow, snakelike writhing movement, especially of the fingers, caused by basal ganglia lesions and seen in Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, movement disorders, p. 533). COMLEX: reduced or absent arm swing and freezing at thresholds also mark the parkinsonian gait (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Highlight the overlap of Parkinson's Disease and Lewy Body dementia","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has had asymmetric parkinsonism treated with levodopa for 9 years. Over the past year he has developed progressive cognitive decline. How is his dementia classified?","choices":{"A":"Corticobasal degeneration","B":"Frontotemporal dementia","C":"Dementia with Lewy bodies","D":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus","E":"Parkinson disease dementia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dementia that appears years after established asymmetric parkinsonism is Parkinson disease dementia, a late secondary complication on the Lewy body spectrum. Corticobasal degeneration is a levodopa-unresponsive tauopathy with strikingly asymmetric cortical signs. Frontotemporal dementia begins with behavior or language change rather than after years of parkinsonism (general knowledge). Dementia with Lewy bodies requires dementia before, with, or within 1 year of motor onset. Normal pressure hydrocephalus brings gait apraxia, incontinence, and cognitive decline without a long levodopa-responsive course (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 3, the one-year rule). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal pressure hydrocephalus in older adults gives the triad of gait apraxia, cognitive dysfunction, and urinary incontinence and is treated with CSF drainage or shunting (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, hydrocephalus, p. 536). COMLEX: dementia complicates long-term survival in Parkinson disease in up to 40% to 80% of patients (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Classify the Atypical Parkinsonian Syndromes and how they differ from PD","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has symmetric rigidity and bradykinesia that did not improve with levodopa, along with fainting on standing and urinary incontinence. MRI shows putaminal atrophy. Damage to which circuit best explains her parkinsonism?","choices":{"A":"Olivopontocerebellar atrophy","B":"Nigrostriatal Lewy pathology","C":"Subthalamic tau tangles","D":"Striatonigral degeneration","E":"Caudate GABAergic loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In multiple system atrophy, striatonigral degeneration produces prominent symmetric parkinsonism that does not respond to levodopa, and autonomic failure explains her fainting and incontinence. Olivopontocerebellar atrophy produces the cerebellar form with ataxia. Nigrostriatal Lewy pathology is the levodopa-responsive lesion of Parkinson disease. Subthalamic tau tangles occur in progressive supranuclear palsy, which has vertical gaze palsy rather than autonomic failure. Caudate GABAergic loss occurs in Huntington disease. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 4, triad of affected circuits in MSA). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the nigrostriatal pathway is one of the dopaminergic pathways blocked by antipsychotics, which is why they cause extrapyramidal symptoms (First Aid Neurology—Anatomy and Physiology, dopaminergic pathways, p. 509). COMLEX: olivopontocerebellar atrophy in multiple system atrophy brings ataxia, dysmetria, and kinetic tremor (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — negative outcomes of impaired mobility and falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the characteristics of Huntington's Disease as well as the genetic characteristics and inheritance","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with Huntington disease is brought in by his sister, who worries about his severe depression. Compared with the general population, his risk of suicide is about what?","choices":{"A":"Twice the rate","B":"Half the rate","C":"The same rate","D":"Ten times the rate","E":"Fifty times the rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Suicide in Huntington disease occurs at about twice the rate of the general population, alongside early personality change, irritability, depression, and impulsive aggression. Half the rate understates the risk. The same rate ignores the excess risk. Ten times and fifty times the rate overstate it. Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 5, neuropsychiatric and behavioral disturbances). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetrabenazine and deutetrabenazine are used to treat the chorea of Huntington disease (First Aid Neurology—Pharmacology, neurodegenerative disease therapy, p. 564). COMLEX: higher executive function, planning, and memory decline insidiously and progress to severe global dementia (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Recognize the shared features as well as differences in different types of Spinocerebellar Degenerative diseases","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with Friedreich ataxia has increased thirst and urination. Which metabolic disorder develops in up to one-quarter of patients with her disease?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism","B":"Diabetes mellitus","C":"Hyperparathyroidism","D":"Adrenal insufficiency","E":"Hemochromatosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes mellitus accompanies Friedreich ataxia in up to 25% of patients. Hypothyroidism is not a recognized systemic complication of the disease (general knowledge). Hyperparathyroidism is not part of it either (general knowledge). Adrenal insufficiency is not associated with it (general knowledge). Hemochromatosis is systemic iron overload, unlike the intramitochondrial iron build-up of frataxin deficiency (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 6, systemic complications of Friedreich ataxia). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Friedreich ataxia is an autosomal recessive GAA repeat disorder on chromosome 9 that impairs the iron-binding mitochondrial protein frataxin and presents in childhood with kyphoscoliosis (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, Friedreich ataxia, p. 545). COMLEX: hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with arrhythmias and heart failure accounts for most deaths before age 40 to 50 (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — changes in the cardiovascular system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Classify features of ALS","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis now has weak intercostal and diaphragmatic muscles. Which complication most often ends the course of his disease?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Hepatic encephalopathy","C":"Respiratory failure","D":"Uremic renal failure","E":"Pressure ulcer sepsis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paralysis of the intercostal and diaphragmatic muscles in ALS leads to respiratory failure, often with recurrent aspiration pneumonia. Cardiac arrhythmia is not the usual terminal event in ALS (general knowledge). Hepatic encephalopathy is unrelated to motor neuron disease (general knowledge). Uremic renal failure is not a feature of ALS (general knowledge). Pressure ulcer sepsis is uncommon because sensation is preserved (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 7 lecture notes (Obj 7, terminal complications). Aligned with SDL 7 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lower motor neuron injury lowers muscle mass, tone, and reflexes and causes fasciculations, whereas upper motor neuron injury raises tone and reflexes (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, effects of motor neuron injury, p. 543). COMLEX: SOD1 mutations, in copper-zinc superoxide dismutase on chromosome 21, account for about 20% of familial ALS (SDL 7 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions affecting elderly individuals","isHighYield":true}],"8":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"A formal diagnosis of hypertension requires averaging properly taken readings from at least how many clinical encounters?","choices":{"A":"One clinic visit","B":"Three separate visits","C":"Two separate visits","D":"Four separate visits","E":"Six separate visits"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diagnosis rests on the average of two or more properly taken readings obtained on two or more separate visits. One clinic visit leaves a transient rise indistinguishable from a sustained one. Three separate visits exceed the stated minimum. Four separate visits also exceed it. Six separate visits exceed it further. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, confirmation and measurement requirements). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thoracic aortic aneurysm is linked to cystic medial degeneration, and its risk factors include hypertension, a bicuspid aortic valve, and connective tissue disease (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: hypertension is defined by sustained arterial pressure elevations measured across multiple clinical encounters (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Heavily calcified, rigid peripheral arteries that resist compression by a standard cuff produce falsely high readings. What is this phenomenon called?","choices":{"A":"Pseudohypertension","B":"White-coat hypertension","C":"Labile hypertension","D":"Auscultatory gap","E":"Resistant hypertension"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pseudohypertension is an artificially raised cuff reading caused by calcified, rigid arteries that resist compression by the cuff. White-coat hypertension is an anxiety-driven rise in clinic readings. Labile hypertension describes wide swings in pressure over time (general knowledge). An auscultatory gap leads to systolic pressure being underestimated rather than overestimated. Resistant hypertension is a true elevation that persists despite multidrug therapy. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, diagnostic pitfalls in older adults). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abdominal or back pain from an aortic aneurysm can signal leaking, dissection, or impending rupture (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline sorts adult blood pressure into four categories, which the 2025 AHA/ACC guideline that replaced it kept unchanged (SDL 8 notes; AHA newsroom, Aug 14 2025).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"During a manual blood pressure measurement, at what rate should the cuff be deflated?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg per second","B":"10 mm Hg per second","C":"2 mm Hg per minute","D":"2 mm Hg per second","E":"20 mm Hg per second"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Proper technique deflates the cuff at 2 mm Hg per second. A rate of 5 mm Hg per second is too fast to time the first Korotkoff sound precisely (general knowledge). A rate of 10 mm Hg per second overshoots the systolic point further (general knowledge). A rate of 2 mm Hg per minute prolongs arterial occlusion and congests the arm (general knowledge). A rate of 20 mm Hg per second makes both readings unreliable (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, proper measurement technique). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: raised intracranial pressure sets off the Cushing reflex, combining hypertension with bradycardia and depressed breathing (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, baroreceptors and chemoreceptors, p. 299). COMLEX: the patient should rest quietly for at least 5 minutes with the arm supported at heart level before measurement (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Long-standing hypertension narrows the deep penetrating arteries that supply the basal ganglia, hemispheric white matter, and brainstem through which vessel-wall change?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrosis","B":"Hyaline arteriolosclerosis","C":"Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis","D":"Monckeberg medial calcification","E":"Amyloid angiopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-standing hypertension causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis of these deep penetrating arteries, and thrombotic occlusion of the narrowed vessels produces lacunar infarcts. Fibrinoid necrosis marks acute arteriolar injury in a hypertensive emergency (general knowledge). Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis is onion-skin thickening seen in severe hypertension (general knowledge). Monckeberg medial calcification affects the media of medium-sized arteries without narrowing the lumen (general knowledge). Amyloid angiopathy deposits amyloid in cortical vessel walls (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, ischemic stroke and lacunar infarctions). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension or diabetes, plasma proteins leak into the subendothelium and thicken small-vessel walls, producing hyaline arteriolosclerosis (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, arteriolosclerosis, p. 306). COMLEX: the deep penetrating arteries affected by small-vessel disease measure about 40 to 900 µm in diameter (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Hypertension accounts for what share of deep intraparenchymal brain hemorrhages?","choices":{"A":"Over half","B":"One-tenth","C":"One-fifth","D":"One-quarter","E":"One-third"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypertension causes over half of deep parenchymal hemorrhages, which arise when rigid arteriolosclerotic vessels rupture. One-tenth understates the share. One-fifth also understates it. One-quarter understates it as well. One-third remains too low. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, intraparenchymal brain hemorrhage). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: systolic dysfunction, or heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, usually follows ischemia, myocardial infarction, or dilated cardiomyopathy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, heart failure, p. 316). COMLEX: midlife hypertension is a documented risk factor for Alzheimer disease (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":1,"stem":"Which funduscopic finding reflects damage to the retinal microcirculation from long-standing hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Cherry-red spot","B":"Drusen deposits","C":"Arteriovenous nicking","D":"Bone spicule pigment","E":"Retinal neovascularization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arteriovenous nicking is a hallmark of hypertensive retinopathy, along with arteriolar narrowing, microaneurysms, flame hemorrhages, and exudates. A cherry-red spot appears with central retinal artery occlusion (general knowledge). Drusen deposits signal age-related macular degeneration (general knowledge). Bone spicule pigment marks retinitis pigmentosa (general knowledge). Retinal neovascularization defines proliferative diabetic retinopathy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, hypertensive retinopathy). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: papilledema in a patient with hypertensive retinopathy marks a hypertensive emergency that calls for immediate lowering of pressure (First Aid Neurology—Ophthalmology, hypertensive retinopathy, p. 552). COMLEX: high intravascular pressure damages the retinal microcirculation and leads to progressive loss of vision (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for about what share of all cases of hypertension?","choices":{"A":"50% to 55%","B":"60% to 65%","C":"70% to 75%","D":"80% to 85%","E":"90% to 95%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary hypertension makes up about 90% to 95% of cases and has no single identifiable cause. A share of 50% to 55% is far too low. A share of 60% to 65% is also too low. A share of 70% to 75% still understates it. A share of 80% to 85% remains below the true figure. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, primary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90% of hypertension is primary and tied to cardiac output or total peripheral resistance, and most of the remainder is secondary to renal or renovascular disease, primary hyperaldosteronism, or obstructive sleep apnea (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: primary hypertension arises from polygenic susceptibility interacting with age, dietary sodium, inactivity, obesity, and insulin resistance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"The label essential hypertension arose from which earlier belief about raised blood pressure?","choices":{"A":"Passed down in families","B":"Needed to perfuse organs","C":"Caused by excess sodium","D":"Benign in older adults","E":"Driven by kidney disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clinicians once believed that raised pressure was a required compensation to guarantee adequate perfusion of vital organs, hence the term essential. Passed down in families describes heritability rather than the origin of the term (general knowledge). Caused by excess sodium names one lifestyle risk factor. Benign in older adults reflects a later, discarded idea of benign hypertension (general knowledge). Driven by kidney disease describes a secondary form. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, historical derivation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cushing syndrome pairs hypertension with hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, truncal obesity, osteoporosis, and easy bruising (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, Cushing syndrome, p. 352). COMLEX: secondary hypertension is directly attributable to an identifiable disorder or an exogenous chemical substance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Resistant hypertension is failure to reach control despite optimal doses of three antihypertensive agents that must include which drug class?","choices":{"A":"Diuretic","B":"Nitrate","C":"Vasodilator","D":"Beta-blocker","E":"Alpha-blocker"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The definition of resistant hypertension requires that one of the three optimally dosed agents be a diuretic. A nitrate is not part of the definition (general knowledge). A direct vasodilator is not required (general knowledge). A beta-blocker is not a required component (general knowledge). An alpha-blocker is not required either (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, clinical suspicion of secondary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism from bilateral adrenal hyperplasia or an aldosterone-secreting adenoma (Conn syndrome) raises aldosterone, suppresses renin, and leaves hypertension resistant to treatment (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, hyperaldosteronism, p. 354). COMLEX: endocrine causes of secondary hypertension are screened with TSH for thyroid disease and with serum calcium and PTH for primary hyperparathyroidism (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"In stiff, aging central arteries, reflected pressure waves return to the aorta at what point in the cardiac cycle?","choices":{"A":"During diastole","B":"At end-diastole","C":"During atrial kick","D":"Between heartbeats","E":"During systole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In rigid aging arteries, reflected waves travel fast and return prematurely during systole, augmenting systolic pressure while diastolic pressure falls. Return during diastole is the pattern of compliant young arteries, where it boosts coronary perfusion. At end-diastole, no newly ejected wave has yet been reflected (general knowledge). Atrial contraction does not time the return of arterial reflected waves (general knowledge). Reflected waves come from each ejection, so they do not arrive between heartbeats (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, premature reflected waves). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac tamponade, aortic stenosis, cardiogenic shock, and advanced heart failure narrow the pulse pressure (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, pulse pressure, p. 290). COMLEX: with loss of the Windkessel effect, stiff central arteries no longer distend in systole and recoil in diastole (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Age-related nephrosclerosis lowers plasma renin activity. The resulting low-renin hypertension responds well to thiazides and to which other drug class?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin receptor blockers","B":"Direct renin inhibitors","C":"Alpha-1 receptor blockers","D":"Calcium channel blockers","E":"Beta-adrenergic blockers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-renin hypertension in older adults responds well to calcium channel blockers along with thiazides. Angiotensin receptor blockers used alone act on a renin-angiotensin system that is less active here. Direct renin inhibitors target renin, which is already low (general knowledge). Alpha-1 receptor blockers are not favored for low-renin hypertension (general knowledge). Beta-adrenergic blockers lower renin release, which adds little when renin is already low (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and calcium channel blockers are the listed agents for primary hypertension (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: aging arteries are less sensitive to beta-adrenergic stimulation, which limits vasodilatory responsiveness (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Hypertension affects more than 70% to 80% of people older than what age?","choices":{"A":"45 years","B":"75 years","C":"55 years","D":"65 years","E":"95 years"},"correct":"B","explanation":"More than 70% to 80% of people older than 75 years have hypertension. An age of 45 years is far too young for that prevalence. An age of 55 years is also too young. An age of 65 years still sets the cutoff too low. An age of 95 years sets it too high. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, advancing chronological age). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension risk rises with age, obesity, diabetes, inactivity, high sodium intake, heavy alcohol use, smoking, and family history, and incidence is highest in Black populations (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: smoking acts synergistically with high blood pressure to multiply cardiovascular, stroke, and mortality risk (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes to the physician for a routine visit. She has had treated hypertension for 20 years and has no symptoms. The nurse inflated the cuff to 150 mm Hg and recorded 138/72 mm Hg; when the physician inflates it to 200 mm Hg, faint sounds begin at 172 mm Hg, vanish at 154 mm Hg, and return at 138 mm Hg. Carotid ultrasonography last year showed calcified plaque with 40% narrowing of the left internal carotid artery (reference: <50% is not hemodynamically significant). Which step would have prevented the nurse's error?","choices":{"A":"Deflating the cuff faster","B":"Using a narrower cuff","C":"Palpating the pulse first","D":"Measuring while standing","E":"Holding the arm lower"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Her readings show an auscultatory gap from arterial stiffness, and checking the pulse obliteration pressure by palpation before auscultating sets the inflation high enough to avoid underestimating systolic pressure. Deflating the cuff faster makes the sounds easier to miss (general knowledge). Using a narrower cuff falsely raises readings and does not expose the gap (general knowledge). Measuring while standing tests for orthostatic change rather than the gap (general knowledge). Holding the arm below heart level falsely raises readings (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, auscultatory gap). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carotid sinus hypersensitivity, set off by carotid massage, shaving, or a tight collar, slows AV conduction and can cause syncope, and it is exaggerated by atherosclerosis and older age (First Aid Cardiovascular—Physiology, baroreceptors and chemoreceptors, p. 299). COMLEX: an appropriately sized bladder cuff should cover 80% of the upper arm (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man comes for a scheduled visit after running out of his antihypertensive medications 10 days ago. He has no headache, chest pain, dyspnea, vision change, or weakness. His blood pressure is 192/112 mm Hg in both arms on repeated readings, and neurologic and funduscopic examinations are normal. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), troponin I is <0.01 ng/mL (reference: <0.04 ng/mL), and the ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy without acute changes. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral titration over weeks","B":"Intravenous labetalol drip","C":"Inpatient admission today","D":"Same-day aortic imaging","E":"Emergency lumbar puncture"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe asymptomatic hypertension, a systolic pressure of 180 mm Hg or more or a diastolic pressure of 110 mm Hg or more without acute target-organ injury, can be managed safely as an outpatient with gradual oral adjustments over days to weeks. An intravenous labetalol drip is parenteral therapy reserved for a hypertensive emergency (general knowledge). Inpatient admission today is needed when acute target-organ injury is present, which he lacks. Same-day aortic imaging is unwarranted without chest or back pain and with equal arm pressures (general knowledge). Emergency lumbar puncture evaluates subarachnoid hemorrhage, which his normal examination and absent headache do not suggest (general knowledge). The notes and Ham's set this cutoff at 180/110 mm Hg; the ACC/AHA cutoff of more than 180 and/or more than 120 mm Hg is general knowledge, and First Aid sets hypertensive urgency at ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg (First Aid p. 304). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, severe elevations); Ham's Ch. 36 (severe asymptomatic hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertensive urgency is severe hypertension, ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg, with no acute end-organ damage (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: a hypertensive emergency mandates emergent inpatient parenteral therapy (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman comes for a wellness visit. Properly taken readings at her last two visits average 134/78 mm Hg and 136/76 mm Hg. She has no diabetes, kidney disease, or cardiovascular disease, and her serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Under the 2017 ACC/AHA categories, how is her blood pressure classified?","choices":{"A":"Normal blood pressure","B":"Elevated blood pressure","C":"Stage 2 hypertension","D":"Severe hypertension","E":"Stage 1 hypertension"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Under the 2017 ACC/AHA categories, a systolic pressure of 130 to 139 mm Hg or a diastolic pressure of 80 to 89 mm Hg is stage 1 hypertension, and the 2025 AHA/ACC guideline that replaced the 2017 one kept the same categories. Normal blood pressure requires a systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg and a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg. Elevated blood pressure is a systolic pressure of 120 to 129 mm Hg with a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg. Stage 2 hypertension starts at 140 mm Hg systolic or 90 mm Hg diastolic. Severe hypertension is a systolic pressure of 180 mm Hg or more or a diastolic pressure of 110 mm Hg or more in the notes and Ham's; the ACC/AHA cutoff of more than 180 and/or more than 120 mm Hg is general knowledge, and First Aid sets hypertensive urgency at ≥ 180/≥ 120 mm Hg (First Aid p. 304). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, diagnostic criteria and staging; severe elevations); Ham's Ch. 36 (Table 36.2, BP categories); 2025 AHA/ACC HBP guideline (AHA newsroom, Aug 14 2025; stage categories unchanged; the severe cutoff is not in the release). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension is a persistent systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or more and/or a diastolic pressure of 80 mm Hg or more (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: JNC 8 advised starting drug therapy at 140/90 mm Hg or higher before age 60 years but at a systolic pressure of 150 mm Hg or higher from age 60 years on, and ambulatory readings predict cardiovascular mortality more strongly than office readings (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after the sudden onset of weakness of his right face, arm, and leg. He has had poorly controlled hypertension for 25 years. Examination shows right hemiparesis with no aphasia, visual field loss, neglect, or sensory deficit. His blood pressure is 178/96 mm Hg, serum glucose is 96 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and MRI shows an 8-mm infarct in the posterior limb of the left internal capsule. Rupture of the same diseased vessels would most likely cause bleeding in which location?","choices":{"A":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","B":"Putaminal hemorrhage","C":"Epidural hematoma","D":"Subdural hematoma","E":"Lobar amyloid hemorrhage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"His pure motor lacunar infarct reflects hypertensive disease of deep penetrating arteries, and rupture of these rigid vessels causes deep parenchymal bleeding, most often in the putamen. Subarachnoid hemorrhage usually follows a ruptured saccular aneurysm (general knowledge). Epidural hematoma results from a torn middle meningeal artery after trauma (general knowledge). Subdural hematoma results from torn bridging veins (general knowledge). Lobar amyloid hemorrhage arises from amyloid in cortical vessels rather than deep penetrating arteries (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, ischemic stroke and intraparenchymal hemorrhage). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertensive bleeds from Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms of the lenticulostriate vessels favor the putamen and globus pallidus and next affect the internal capsule, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intracranial hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: thrombotic occlusion of narrowed deep arterioles leaves small cavitary lacunar infarcts (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 4 months of exertional dyspnea and ankle swelling. She has had hypertension for 30 years, often poorly controlled. Examination shows bibasilar crackles, an S4 gallop, and 1+ pitting ankle edema. Echocardiography shows concentric left ventricular hypertrophy with an ejection fraction of 62% (reference: 52–72%), and serum BNP is 420 pg/mL (reference: <100 pg/mL). Which abnormality most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Loss of myofibrillar elements","B":"Dilated ventricular chamber","C":"Mitral regurgitant volume","D":"Impaired diastolic filling","E":"Pericardial constriction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hypertension produces heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, in which a stiff, thickened ventricle impairs diastolic filling. Loss of myofibrillar elements follows prolonged overload and leads to a reduced ejection fraction, which she does not have. A dilated ventricular chamber accompanies systolic failure rather than concentric hypertrophy (general knowledge). Mitral regurgitant volume would appear on echocardiography as a regurgitant jet (general knowledge). Pericardial constriction does not thicken the ventricular wall (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, concentric LVH and heart failure). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diastolic dysfunction, or heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, lowers ventricular compliance and often stems from myocardial hypertrophy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, heart failure, p. 316). COMLEX: sustained afterload makes myocardial cells enlarge, thickening the left ventricular wall as a compensatory adaptation (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — effects of diastolic dysfunction in the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for follow-up of chronic kidney disease. He has had hypertension for 30 years and does not have diabetes. His estimated GFR has fallen from 62 to 38 mL/min/1.73 m² over 6 years (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and the urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 140 mg/g (reference: <30 mg/g). Ultrasonography shows small, symmetric kidneys with finely granular cortical surfaces, and biopsy shows hyaline thickening of the afferent arterioles. Through which mechanism does this arteriolar lesion lower his GFR?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition","B":"Podocyte foot effacement","C":"Tubular crystal obstruction","D":"Glomerular basement splitting","E":"Reduced glomerular perfusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyaline thickening of afferent arterioles and interlobular arteries compromises glomerular perfusion, driving loss of functioning nephrons and a falling GFR. Immune complex deposition defines immune-mediated glomerulonephritis (general knowledge). Podocyte foot effacement underlies nephrotic syndromes such as minimal change disease (general knowledge). Tubular crystal obstruction follows crystal-forming drugs or urate (general knowledge). Glomerular basement splitting characterizes Alport syndrome (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, nephrosclerosis). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertension is the strongest risk factor for aortic dissection, a longitudinal intimal tear that creates a false lumen (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic dissection, p. 307). COMLEX: nephrosclerosis speeds chronic kidney disease and its progression toward end-stage renal disease (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — factors influencing organ functional performance with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man comes for follow-up 1 week after lisinopril was added for blood pressure above goal on amlodipine and chlorthalidone. He has a 50-pack-year smoking history and claudication of both calves. His blood pressure is 176/88 mm Hg, there is a periumbilical bruit, and pedal pulses are diminished. Serum creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 1.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). If the suspected lesion is confirmed, how does medical therapy compare with revascularization?","choices":{"A":"Worse BP control","B":"More cardiac deaths","C":"Faster renal decline","D":"Comparable outcomes","E":"More hospitalizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor in an older smoker with vascular disease suggests atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis, since a rise of 0.5 to 1.0 mg/dL or more raises that suspicion, and medical management is at least as effective as surgical revascularization. Worse BP control is not seen with medical therapy in this comparison. More cardiac deaths do not follow medical management. Faster renal decline is not expected with medical therapy. More hospitalizations are not a recognized drawback of medical therapy (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, renovascular disease and the creatinine rise after ACE inhibitor or ARB); Ham's Ch. 36 (renal artery stenosis, creatinine rise of 0.5 to 1 mg/dL or more). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in renovascular disease, renal ischemia increases renin release, and the angiotensin II and aldosterone that follow raise blood pressure (First Aid Renal—Pathology, renovascular disease, p. 623). COMLEX: glomerulonephritis and advanced chronic kidney disease are the renal parenchymal causes of secondary hypertension (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — stable ischemic heart disease and acute coronary syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man comes to the physician because his blood pressure remains high despite full doses of amlodipine, lisinopril, and chlorthalidone. His wife reports loud snoring with pauses in his breathing, and he falls asleep while reading. His BMI is 36 kg/m², neck circumference is 46 cm, and blood pressure is 162/94 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL), and calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which mechanism most likely sustains his daytime hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Mineralocorticoid excess","B":"Sympathetic overactivation","C":"Catecholamine-secreting tumor","D":"Renal artery narrowing","E":"Glucocorticoid excess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Obstructive sleep apnea causes chronic nocturnal hypoxemia and sympathetic overactivation that sustain daytime hypertension. Mineralocorticoid excess often lowers serum potassium, which is normal here (general knowledge). A catecholamine-secreting tumor causes episodic headaches, sweating, and palpitations (general knowledge). Renal artery narrowing would be suggested by a creatinine rise after an ACE inhibitor. Glucocorticoid excess brings truncal obesity with striae and easy bruising (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, obstructive sleep apnea). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive sleep apnea is respiratory effort against a blocked airway, linked to obesity, loud snoring, and daytime sleepiness, and treated with weight loss and CPAP (First Aid Respiratory—Pathology, obstructive sleep apnea, p. 697). COMLEX: secondary hypertension is unlikely to appear as a new problem in older adults (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — pulmonary conditions affecting elderly individuals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman comes for a blood pressure check. Her pressure had been 128/76 mm Hg on one antihypertensive drug for 5 years, but it is now 156/88 mm Hg on two visits. Six weeks ago she began taking naproxen twice daily for knee osteoarthritis, and she drinks one glass of wine weekly. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), potassium is 4.4 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which step is most likely to restore her previous control?","choices":{"A":"Stopping the NSAID","B":"Adding a thiazide","C":"Adding a beta-blocker","D":"Reducing salt intake","E":"Ordering renal imaging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs raise blood pressure, so stopping naproxen removes the cause of her loss of control. Adding a thiazide treats the rise but leaves its cause in place (general knowledge). Adding a beta-blocker also adds a drug without removing the cause (general knowledge). Reducing salt intake helps but does not address the new drug (general knowledge). Ordering renal imaging is unwarranted with a normal creatinine and an evident drug cause (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, medication-induced and exogenous substances). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperparathyroidism, usually from an adenoma or hyperplasia, causes hypercalcemia, kidney stones, and hypophosphatemia and is often asymptomatic (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, hyperparathyroidism, p. 349). COMLEX: systemic corticosteroids, sympathomimetic decongestants, and excess alcohol also raise blood pressure (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — risk factors for adverse drug reactions in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes for a routine visit. She has no symptoms, does not have diabetes, and takes no medications. Her blood pressure is 172/68 mm Hg in both arms, a pulse pressure of 104 mm Hg. Carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity is 14 m/s (reference: <10 m/s), and echocardiography shows normal valves. Which structural change in her aortic wall best explains this pressure pattern?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle hyperplasia","B":"Fragmented elastin fibers","C":"Intimal lipid streaks","D":"Intimal fibrin thrombi","E":"Adventitial lymphocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isolated systolic hypertension, a systolic pressure of 140 mm Hg or more with a diastolic pressure below 90 mm Hg, with a wide pulse pressure reflects a stiffer aorta, in which elastin degenerates, frays, and fragments while collagen accumulates and cross-links. The ACC/AHA guideline instead defines isolated systolic hypertension as a systolic pressure of 130 mm Hg or more with a diastolic pressure below 80 mm Hg (general knowledge); the notes' definition is used here. Smooth muscle hyperplasia narrows small arterioles in severe hypertension rather than stiffening the aorta (general knowledge). Intimal lipid streaks are early atherosclerosis and do not raise pulse wave velocity (general knowledge). Intimal fibrin thrombi form on disrupted plaques and occlude vessels (general knowledge). Adventitial lymphocytes suggest aortitis (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, ISH definition; vascular remodeling and arterial stiffening). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: elastin gets its stretch from extracellular cross-linking by lysyl oxidase and is broken down by elastase, which α1-antitrypsin normally inhibits (First Aid Biochemistry—Cellular, elastin, p. 50). COMLEX: a widened pulse pressure predicts coronary disease and stroke better than mean arterial or diastolic pressure (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man is enrolled in a vascular aging study. He has hypertension, does not have diabetes, and does not smoke. Brachial ultrasonography shows flow-mediated dilation of 2% (reference: >6%), while dilation after sublingual nitroglycerin is normal. Fasting glucose is 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and LDL cholesterol is 96 mg/dL (reference: <100 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most likely accounts for his impaired flow-mediated response?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial NO synthase","B":"Cyclooxygenase-1","C":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme","D":"Soluble guanylyl cyclase","E":"Prostacyclin synthase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aging arteries have diminished endothelial nitric oxide synthase activity and less bioavailable nitric oxide, impairing flow-mediated vasodilation. Cyclooxygenase-1 makes thromboxane and prostaglandins rather than the flow-mediated nitric oxide signal (general knowledge). Angiotensin-converting enzyme produces a vasoconstrictor and does not mediate this dilation (general knowledge). Soluble guanylyl cyclase must be intact because nitroglycerin still dilates the artery (general knowledge). Prostacyclin synthase does not explain a selective loss of flow-mediated dilation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, endothelial dysfunction). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nitric oxide relaxes smooth muscle, including the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid Gastrointestinal—Physiology, gastrointestinal regulatory substances, p. 378). COMLEX: reactive oxygen species add oxidative injury that further impairs endothelium-dependent vasodilation (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — proposed causes of cellular aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman comes to the physician because of 2 months of light-headedness when she gets out of bed. She has hypertension treated with amlodipine and takes no other medications. Supine blood pressure is 158/80 mm Hg with a pulse of 72/min; after 3 minutes of standing, it is 128/72 mm Hg with a pulse of 76/min. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12–16 g/dL), and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Which age-related change most likely explains her findings?","choices":{"A":"Plasma volume expansion","B":"Excess renin secretion","C":"Blunted baroreflex","D":"Increased beta sensitivity","E":"Heightened alpha-1 tone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reduced baroreceptor reflex sensitivity blunts the heart-rate and vascular response to standing, causing orthostatic hypotension and labile pressures. Plasma volume expansion would protect against an orthostatic fall (general knowledge). Excess renin secretion runs counter to the age-related decline in renin. Increased beta sensitivity runs counter to the age-related loss of beta-adrenergic responsiveness. Heightened alpha-1 tone would support standing pressure (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pheochromocytoma, the most common adrenal medullary tumor in adults, arises from neural crest chromaffin cells and causes episodic hypertension (First Aid Endocrine—Pathology, pheochromocytoma, p. 355). COMLEX: chronic emotional stress significantly increases systemic vascular resistance (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 3 — multifactorial physiologic factors contributing to falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has clinic readings near 126/78 mm Hg, but his validated home monitor averages 148/92 mm Hg over 2 weeks. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"White-coat hypertension","B":"Orthostatic hypotension","C":"Labile hypertension","D":"Masked hypertension","E":"Resistant hypertension"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Masked hypertension is a nonhypertensive clinic reading (<130/80 mm Hg) that hides a higher usual pressure, which out-of-office monitoring reveals. White-coat hypertension is the reverse, with raised clinic readings and normal home readings. Orthostatic hypotension is a fall in pressure on standing rather than a clinic-home mismatch (general knowledge). Labile hypertension describes wide swings over time (general knowledge). Resistant hypertension is uncontrolled pressure despite multidrug therapy. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, out-of-office confirmation; BP categories). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tobacco smoking is the strongest risk factor for abdominal aortic aneurysm, which involves transmural inflammation and breakdown of extracellular matrix (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, aortic aneurysm, p. 306). COMLEX: ambulatory and home monitoring are the out-of-office methods used to confirm the diagnosis (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — rationale for treating hypertension in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has an irregularly irregular pulse, and her ECG shows no P waves. Which hypertension-driven change most promotes this rhythm?","choices":{"A":"Aortic root dilation","B":"Mitral valve prolapse","C":"Left atrial dilation","D":"Sinus node fibrosis","E":"Pericardial effusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High ventricular filling pressures dilate the left atrium and promote atrial hypertrophy and fibrosis, raising the incidence of atrial fibrillation. Aortic root dilation affects the aortic valve rather than atrial rhythm (general knowledge). Mitral valve prolapse is a degenerative valve disorder unrelated to hypertension (general knowledge). Sinus node fibrosis causes bradyarrhythmias such as sick sinus syndrome (general knowledge). Pericardial effusion lowers QRS voltage rather than driving atrial fibrillation (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, atrial remodeling and arrhythmias). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lacunar infarcts of the lenticulostriate arteries cause pure motor strokes without cortical signs and stem from lipohyalinosis in unmanaged hypertension (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, effects of strokes, p. 526). COMLEX: hypertension accelerates coronary atherosclerosis, predisposing to stable angina and acute coronary syndromes (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — risk factors for atrial fibrillation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Discuss the effect that hypertension may potentially have on other organ systems","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing hypertension reports gradually worsening difficulty maintaining erections, which his physician attributes to his hypertension. Which mechanism links the two?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone","B":"Endothelial injury","C":"Prolactin excess","D":"Pelvic nerve injury","E":"Psychogenic anxiety"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Endothelial injury and arterial insufficiency in the penile vascular bed explain the high prevalence of erectile dysfunction with hypertension. Low testosterone is a hormonal cause unrelated to his hypertension (general knowledge). Prolactin excess suppresses gonadotropins and is not a vascular effect (general knowledge). Pelvic nerve injury follows pelvic surgery or radiation (general knowledge). Psychogenic anxiety is not a vascular consequence of hypertension (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 2, peripheral vasculature and sexual function). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blocker adverse effects include erectile dysfunction, bradycardia, AV block, and masked hypoglycemia (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: systemic arterial remodeling predisposes to peripheral arterial disease, claudication, and diminished distal pulses (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — antihypertensive classes, mechanisms, and adverse effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man whose blood pressure was controlled on one drug for 15 years has a sharp rise to 184/96 mm Hg over 2 months despite confirmed adherence. What does this change most suggest?","choices":{"A":"Secondary cause","B":"White-coat effect","C":"Normal aging","D":"Salt sensitivity","E":"Cuff artifact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A sudden, acute rise in pressure after years of stable control should prompt a search for a secondary cause. A white-coat effect raises clinic readings from the start rather than producing a new sustained rise (general knowledge). Normal aging raises pressure gradually over years. Salt sensitivity does not explain an abrupt change after years of control (general knowledge). Cuff artifact such as pseudohypertension is a long-standing measurement problem rather than a new rise. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 3, clinical suspicion of secondary hypertension). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, with onion-skin proliferation of smooth muscle cells, accompanies severe hypertension (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, arteriolosclerosis, p. 306). COMLEX: atherosclerotic narrowing of the renal arteries is the common renovascular cause in older adults (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman notes that her home blood pressure rises by about 15 mm Hg after salty holiday meals. Which age-related change best explains this response?","choices":{"A":"Increased aldosterone secretion","B":"Enhanced intestinal sodium uptake","C":"Elevated plasma renin activity","D":"Reduced renal sodium handling","E":"Heightened thirst and intake"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Aging kidneys have a diminished capacity to handle sodium loads, so older adults show pronounced salt-sensitive rises in pressure. Increased aldosterone secretion is not an age-related change (general knowledge). Enhanced intestinal sodium uptake does not develop with age (general knowledge). Elevated plasma renin activity runs counter to the age-related fall in renin. Heightened thirst and intake runs counter to the blunted thirst of older adults (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, dietary sodium sensitivity). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fibromuscular dysplasia gives the renal artery a string-of-beads look and usually affects adult women (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pathology, hypertension, p. 304). COMLEX: adiposity expands intravascular volume, increases sympathetic outflow, and drives hyperinsulinemia (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — nonpharmacologic treatment of hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old Black man asks how the stroke risk of Black adults in the United States compares with that of White adults. About how much higher is it for Black adults as a population?","choices":{"A":"10% higher","B":"20% higher","C":"50% higher","D":"30% higher","E":"75% higher"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Black individuals in the United States have roughly a 50% higher stroke risk than White individuals, along with earlier onset and more severe hypertension. A figure of 10% higher understates it. A figure of 20% higher also understates it. A figure of 30% higher still falls short. A figure of 75% higher overstates it. Sources: SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, racial disparities). Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in older adults, cerebral amyloid angiopathy produces repeated lobar hemorrhagic strokes (First Aid Neurology—Pathology, intracranial hemorrhage, p. 528). COMLEX: regular aerobic and resistance exercise lowers systolic pressure, whereas inactivity speeds arterial stiffening (SDL 8 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — influences of genetics versus lifestyle in aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differential between primary and secondary hypertension","batch":5,"stem":"A 77-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. His blood pressure has stayed near 158/84 mm Hg over three visits despite prescriptions for full doses of amlodipine, lisinopril, and chlorthalidone. He does not snore, his BMI is 25 kg/m², and serum potassium is 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with a creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Before further testing, which cause should be addressed first because it is the most common cause of this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary aldosteronism","B":"Renal artery stenosis","C":"Obstructive sleep apnea","D":"Pheochromocytoma","E":"Medication nonadherence"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His 158/84 mm Hg is stage 2 hypertension on three optimally dosed drugs including a diuretic, which is resistant hypertension, and resistant hypertension is most often caused by nonadherence to the prescribed medications. Primary aldosteronism often lowers potassium, which is normal here (general knowledge). Renal artery stenosis is suggested by a creatinine rise after ACE inhibition, which he has not had. Obstructive sleep apnea is less likely without snoring or obesity (general knowledge). Pheochromocytoma causes episodic spells and is rare (general knowledge). Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Resistant Hypertension; the leading cause is not in the SDL 8 notes); SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 1, BP categories; Obj 3, resistant hypertension). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 8 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome, an autosomal dominant gain-of-function defect that slows breakdown of the epithelial Na+ channel, causes hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and low aldosterone (First Aid Renal—Physiology, renal tubular defects, p. 604). COMLEX: patients who need four or more antihypertensive drugs for control also meet the definition of resistant hypertension (Ham's Ch. 36, Resistant Hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — obstacles to successful hypertension treatment in older adults","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the pathophysiology and risk factors for hypertension in the elderly","batch":5,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with hypertension comes for follow-up. Her estimated GFR is 48 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and her urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio is 22 mg/g (reference: <30 mg/g). Age-related hormonal changes, together with her reduced kidney function, raise her risk of which electrolyte disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Hypernatremia","B":"Hyperkalemia","C":"Hypokalemia","D":"Hypercalcemia","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Declining aldosterone with age, especially alongside reduced renal function, raises the risk of hyperkalemia. Hypernatremia results from water loss with impaired thirst (general knowledge). Hypokalemia follows aldosterone excess or diuretic use, the opposite situation (general knowledge). Hypercalcemia points to hyperparathyroidism or malignancy (general knowledge). Hypomagnesemia follows diuretics, alcohol use, or gastrointestinal loss (general knowledge). Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Risk Factors and Pathophysiology; the aldosterone decline and potassium risk are not in the SDL 8 notes); SDL 8 lecture notes (Obj 4, altered neurohormonal regulation). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 8 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 8 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 renal tubular acidosis follows hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, and the hyperkalemia it causes lowers ammonia excretion and produces metabolic acidosis (First Aid Renal—Physiology, renal tubular acidosis, p. 611). COMLEX: the vascular changes of aging contribute more to hypertension than the age-related fall in renin activity (Ham's Ch. 36, Risk Factors and Pathophysiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — antihypertensive classes, mechanisms, and adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"}],"9":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Among hypertensive adults aged 60 years and older, what share achieve adequate blood pressure control?","choices":{"A":"30%","B":"50%","C":"60%","D":"71%","E":"77%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only about 30% of hypertensive adults aged 60 years and older reach adequate control. A share of 50% overstates control. A share of 60% overstates it further. A share of 71% is the proportion receiving drug treatment. A share of 77% is the proportion aware of their hypertension. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, epidemiology of control rates). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blockers lower blood pressure by reducing cardiac output and by blocking β1 receptors on juxtaglomerular cells, which cuts renin secretion (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: control rates are lower among Black, Asian American, and Hispanic adults than among White adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician hesitates to intensify antihypertensive therapy in an older adult for fear of adverse drug events, falls, or acute kidney injury. What is this obstacle called?","choices":{"A":"Clinical equipoise","B":"Diagnostic momentum","C":"Defensive medicine","D":"Prescribing cascade","E":"Therapeutic inertia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Therapeutic inertia is a clinician's reluctance to intensify therapy despite uncontrolled blood pressure. Clinical equipoise is genuine uncertainty about which treatment is better, the ethical basis for a trial (general knowledge). Diagnostic momentum is the carrying forward of an early diagnosis without reconsidering it (general knowledge). Defensive medicine is testing or treatment ordered to avoid liability (general knowledge). A prescribing cascade is treating a drug's adverse effect with another drug (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, clinician and health system obstacles). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with diabetes, β-blockers can mask the symptoms of hypoglycemia (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: guideline discrepancies, such as the JNC 8 systolic goal below 150 mm Hg at age 60 years or older versus the ACC/AHA goal below 130 mm Hg, leave clinicians unsure how hard to titrate (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":1,"stem":"Which physiologic change makes systolic blood pressure in older adults intrinsically resistant to antihypertensive therapy?","choices":{"A":"Heightened renin activity","B":"Excess sympathetic tone","C":"Expanded plasma volume","D":"Central arterial stiffening","E":"Rapid hepatic drug clearance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Age-associated loss of central arterial compliance and marked vascular stiffening make systolic pressure intrinsically hard to lower. Heightened renin activity is the opposite of the age-related fall in renin (general knowledge). Excess sympathetic tone is not the stated cause of treatment resistance in older adults (general knowledge). Expanded plasma volume is not an age-related cause of resistance (general knowledge). Rapid hepatic drug clearance runs counter to the slower drug clearance of older adults (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, pharmacokinetic and physiological obstacles). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fenoldopam is a dopamine D1 receptor agonist that dilates coronary, peripheral, renal, and splanchnic vessels and promotes natriuresis (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, fenoldopam, p. 323). COMLEX: older adults are more susceptible to drug accumulation, volume depletion, and severe orthostatic hypotension (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"Large clinical trials show that drug treatment of hypertension in older adults reduces the incidence of stroke and cardiovascular disease by about how much?","choices":{"A":"5% to 10%","B":"30% to 40%","C":"10% to 15%","D":"50% to 60%","E":"70% to 80%"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pharmacologic lowering of blood pressure cuts stroke and cardiovascular disease by 30% to 40% in older adults. A reduction of 5% to 10% understates the benefit. A reduction of 10% to 15% also understates it. A reduction of 50% to 60% overstates it. A reduction of 70% to 80% overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, cardiovascular and cerebrovascular morbidity). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nitroprusside is a short-acting vasodilator of arteries and veins that raises cGMP by releasing nitric oxide and can cause cyanide toxicity (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, nitroprusside, p. 323). COMLEX: because baseline risk rises with age, older adults gain large absolute risk reductions from lowering blood pressure (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"In the trial of intensive systolic control below 120 mm Hg, how many patients in the ≥75-year subgroup needed treatment over 3 years to prevent one composite cardiovascular event?","choices":{"A":"27","B":"7","C":"12","D":"54","E":"81"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intensive control in SPRINT had a number needed to treat of 27 over 3 years in the ≥75-year subgroup for the composite of myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, or cardiovascular death. A value of 7 overstates the benefit. A value of 12 also overstates it. A value of 54 understates it. A value of 81 understates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, SPRINT). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dihydropyridines nicardipine and clevidipine are used for hypertensive urgency or emergency (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: SPRINT compared a systolic goal below 120 mm Hg with standard control below 140 mm Hg and included a prespecified subgroup aged 75 years and older (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":1,"stem":"Thiazide diuretics lower the risk of hip fracture by about one-third in older adults. Which mechanism explains this benefit?","choices":{"A":"Increased vitamin D activation","B":"Lower parathyroid hormone","C":"Reduced urinary calcium loss","D":"Enhanced intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Fewer orthostatic falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides reduce urinary calcium excretion, which protects against osteoporosis and lowers hip fracture risk. Increased vitamin D activation is not how thiazides act (general knowledge). Lower parathyroid hormone is not the stated mechanism (general knowledge). Enhanced intestinal calcium uptake is a vitamin D effect rather than a thiazide effect (general knowledge). Fewer orthostatic falls are unlikely, because thiazides can cause volume depletion and hypotension. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides inhibit NaCl reabsorption in the early distal convoluted tubule and lower calcium excretion, so they are also used for idiopathic hypercalciuria and osteoporosis (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, thiazide diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: a heart-healthy DASH-style eating pattern lowers systolic pressure by about 11 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"Limiting dietary sodium to no more than 2.3 to 2.4 g per day lowers systolic blood pressure by about how much?","choices":{"A":"1 mm Hg","B":"5 mm Hg","C":"11 mm Hg","D":"15 mm Hg","E":"20 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium restriction to 2.3 to 2.4 g per day lowers systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg. A fall of 1 mm Hg understates the effect. A fall of 11 mm Hg is the effect of a heart-healthy DASH-type diet. A fall of 15 mm Hg overstates the effect. A fall of 20 mm Hg overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, dietary sodium restriction). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cortical collecting tubule, spironolactone and eplerenone competitively block the aldosterone receptor, while triamterene and amiloride block Na+ channels there (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, potassium-sparing diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: older adults, Black individuals, and patients with chronic kidney disease or diabetes show the largest pressure falls with sodium restriction (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"The DASH eating plan is not recommended for patients whose chronic kidney disease has reached stage 3 or 4 because it is rich in which minerals?","choices":{"A":"Calcium and magnesium","B":"Bicarbonate and chloride","C":"Iron and vitamin B12","D":"Phosphorus and potassium","E":"Magnesium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The DASH diet is rich in potassium and phosphorus, which accumulate in advanced chronic kidney disease. Calcium and magnesium content is not the stated concern (general knowledge). Bicarbonate and chloride are not the minerals that limit its use (general knowledge). Iron and vitamin B12 are not the concern (general knowledge). Magnesium and sulfate are not the concern either (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, DASH eating plan clinical caveat). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: potassium-sparing diuretics can cause hyperkalemia that leads to arrhythmias, and spironolactone adds endocrine effects such as gynecomastia (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, potassium-sparing diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: the DASH plan emphasizes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, poultry, fish, nuts, and low-fat dairy while restricting saturated fats, red meat, and sweets (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":1,"stem":"To lower systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg, older adults should do at least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity activity such as brisk walking on at least how many days per week?","choices":{"A":"1 day","B":"2 days","C":"3 days","D":"4 days","E":"5 days"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At least 30 minutes of moderate-intensity activity on at least 5 days per week, about 150 minutes per week, lowers systolic pressure by about 5 mm Hg; Ham's says only at least 30 minutes per day. One day per week is far below the recommendation. Two days per week also falls short. Three days per week still falls short. Four days per week remains one day short. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, aerobic physical activity); 2017 ACC/AHA guideline (90 to 150 minutes per week, general knowledge); Ham's Ch. 36 (Table 36.4). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-blockers lower mortality after myocardial infarction, slow AV conduction in supraventricular tachycardia, and help prevent variceal bleeding (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: exercise also improves cardiovascular conditioning, preserves muscle mass, and reduces fall risk (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"Chlorthalidone and hydrochlorothiazide lower blood pressure by inhibiting which transporter in the distal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Epithelial sodium channel","B":"Sodium-hydrogen exchanger","C":"Sodium-chloride symporter","D":"Sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Sodium-potassium ATPase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics inhibit the sodium-chloride symporter in the distal convoluted tubule, reducing intravascular volume and then peripheral resistance. The epithelial sodium channel is blocked by amiloride and triamterene in the collecting tubule (general knowledge). The sodium-hydrogen exchanger of the proximal tubule is not their target (general knowledge). The sodium-glucose cotransporter is blocked by SGLT2 inhibitors (general knowledge). Sodium-potassium ATPase is inhibited by digoxin (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, thiazide and thiazide-like diuretics). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics block the Na+/K+/2Cl− cotransporter of the thick ascending limb and can cause ototoxicity, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, dehydration, and gout (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, loop diuretics, p. 626). COMLEX: older adults should receive low thiazide doses rather than maximal diuretic doses (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"The dry cough caused by ACE inhibitors results from accumulation of which mediator?","choices":{"A":"Bradykinin","B":"Histamine","C":"Angiotensin II","D":"Leukotriene C4","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown, and the accumulated bradykinin causes a dry cough. Histamine release drives allergic reactions rather than this cough (general knowledge). Angiotensin II falls rather than accumulates with ACE inhibition. Leukotriene C4 mediates bronchoconstriction in asthma (general knowledge). Serotonin is not the mediator of this cough (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, ACE inhibitors). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARBs block angiotensin II binding at the AT1 receptor and do not raise bradykinin, so they suit patients intolerant of ACE inhibitors (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, angiotensin II receptor blockers, p. 628). COMLEX: ACE inhibitors also cause angioedema, rash, an acute fall in GFR, and altered taste (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":1,"stem":"Diltiazem and verapamil are contraindicated in which condition?","choices":{"A":"Atrial fibrillation","B":"Systolic heart failure","C":"Vasospastic angina","D":"Essential tremor","E":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers have negative inotropic effects and are contraindicated in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Atrial fibrillation is an indication for these drugs as rate control (general knowledge). Vasospastic angina responds to calcium channel blockers (general knowledge). Essential tremor is not a contraindication (general knowledge). Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can be treated with verapamil (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, nondihydropyridines). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nondihydropyridines can cause cardiac depression, AV block, and constipation, and verapamil can raise prolactin (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: diltiazem and verapamil slow SA node automaticity and prolong the AV node refractory period (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman comes for follow-up of hypertension with her son. She takes four antihypertensive drugs, one each morning, two at morning and bedtime, and one three times a day, and pill counts show many missed doses. She lives alone and has mild cognitive impairment with a Montreal Cognitive Assessment score of 22/30 (normal: ≥26/30). Her blood pressure is 162/84 mm Hg, and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Which change is most likely to improve her blood pressure control?","choices":{"A":"Adding a fifth antihypertensive","B":"Switching to brand-name drugs","C":"Increasing visit frequency","D":"Childproof pill containers","E":"Once-daily consolidated regimen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Multiple prescriptions with differing dosing intervals foster confusion and forgetfulness, especially with mild cognitive decline, so consolidating to once-daily dosing targets her barrier. Adding a fifth antihypertensive worsens the complexity that drives her missed doses. Switching to brand-name drugs adds cost without simplifying dosing (general knowledge). Increasing visit frequency does not fix the daily dosing confusion (general knowledge). Childproof pill containers add a physical barrier for older adults. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, complex regimens and cognitive impairment). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-methyldopa is used for hypertension in pregnancy and can cause a positive direct Coombs test with hemolysis, drug-induced lupus, and hyperprolactinemia (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α2-agonists, p. 243). COMLEX: most older hypertensive adults need two or more medications to reach goal (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old man comes for follow-up because his blood pressure has risen from 132/78 to 158/86 mm Hg over 3 months. He has severe hand osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes, and his grip strength is markedly reduced. He says two of his newly refilled pill bottles are still full because the lids hurt his hands to open, although he reads the labels without difficulty. His serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL) and potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which intervention best addresses the barrier to his adherence?","choices":{"A":"Large-print bottle labels","B":"Easy-open container caps","C":"Liquid drug formulations","D":"Pill-crushing device","E":"Generic substitution"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Arthritis hinders opening childproof containers, so non-childproof, easy-open caps remove his barrier. Large-print bottle labels help visual impairment, which he does not have. Liquid drug formulations help patients who have trouble swallowing large tablets. A pill-crushing device also targets swallowing rather than opening (general knowledge). Generic substitution targets cost rather than access to the pills (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, physical disabilities and formulations). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydralazine, methyldopa, labetalol, and nifedipine are the antihypertensives listed for hypertension in pregnancy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: reduced salivation makes large tablets difficult for older adults to swallow (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man comes for a routine visit 4 months after hypertension was diagnosed during an evaluation for morning headaches. His headaches resolved within weeks of starting treatment, and he stopped his medications soon after because he felt well. His blood pressure is now 168/92 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and an ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy. Which factor most likely explains his nonadherence?","choices":{"A":"Resolution of symptoms","B":"Fear of drug interactions","C":"Distrust of generic drugs","D":"Belief in herbal remedies","E":"Concern about addiction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because hypertension is usually silent, patients often stop treatment when they feel well or when their initial symptoms resolve. Fear of drug interactions is not suggested by his history (general knowledge). Distrust of generic drugs is not suggested either (general knowledge). Belief in herbal remedies is not described (general knowledge). Concern about addiction does not fit antihypertensive drugs or his account (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, asymptomatic nature of disease). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aliskiren is a direct renin inhibitor that blocks conversion of angiotensinogen to angiotensin I (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, aliskiren, p. 628). COMLEX: office readings can exceed ambulatory or home readings by up to 19/11 mm Hg because of anxiety (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — effects of hypertension on other organ systems","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman comes for follow-up of untreated hypertension. Her blood pressure has averaged 172/80 mm Hg over three visits, she lives independently, and she has no history of stroke or coronary disease. Her estimated GFR is 58 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Her physician cites a placebo-controlled trial of adults aged 80 years or older, mean age 84, treated with indapamide with or without perindopril to a target of 150/80 mm Hg. In that trial, which outcome showed the largest relative reduction at 2 years?","choices":{"A":"Fatal stroke","B":"Overall mortality","C":"Heart failure","D":"Myocardial infarction","E":"Hip fracture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In HYVET, active treatment cut congestive heart failure by 64%, the largest reduction among its outcomes. Fatal stroke fell by 39%. Overall mortality fell by 21%. Myocardial infarction was not among the reported reductions. Hip fracture was not a reported HYVET outcome. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, HYVET). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with heart failure, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, β-blockers for compensated failure, aldosterone antagonists for mortality benefit, and diuretics for symptoms are used (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: in HYVET, fatal or nonfatal stroke fell by 30% and cardiovascular death by 23% (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — effects of diastolic dysfunction in the aging heart","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. His systolic pressure is 146 mm Hg on two drugs, and he has no diabetes or prior stroke. He walks daily, and his estimated GFR is 64 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²) with a potassium of 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). His physician proposes adding a third drug to aim for a systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg rather than below 140 mm Hg. In the trial that tested this target, what relative reduction in composite cardiovascular outcomes did it achieve in the ≥75-year subgroup?","choices":{"A":"10%","B":"21%","C":"39%","D":"64%","E":"33%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intensive control in SPRINT produced a 33% relative risk reduction in the ≥75-year subgroup in fatal and nonfatal myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, or cardiovascular death. A reduction of 10% understates the benefit. A reduction of 21% is the HYVET fall in all-cause mortality. A reduction of 39% is the HYVET fall in stroke mortality. A reduction of 64% is the HYVET fall in heart failure. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, SPRINT). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nebivolol combines β1-selective blockade with β3 stimulation that activates nitric oxide synthase in the vasculature (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: SPRINT's composite outcome counted myocardial infarction, stroke, heart failure, and cardiovascular death (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — impact of age and multimorbidity on coronary disease outcomes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes to the physician because his wife has noticed 1 year of slowed thinking and trouble managing their finances. He has had poorly controlled hypertension since his 40s. His MoCA screening score is 23/30 (normal: ≥26/30), vitamin B12 is 480 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and TSH is 1.9 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). MRI shows confluent periventricular white matter hyperintensities and two small lacunes in the basal ganglia. Lowering his blood pressure is most likely to reduce the risk of which outcome?","choices":{"A":"Progressive dementia","B":"Frontotemporal degeneration","C":"Prion-mediated dementia","D":"Lewy body pathology","E":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypertension drives microvascular damage, ischemic white matter injury, and lacunar strokes that underlie vascular dementia and contribute to Alzheimer disease. In treatment trials, Syst-Eur (with SHEP) showed only a modest trend toward fewer dementia cases, PROGRESS reduced dementia in patients with prior cerebrovascular disease, and HYVET-COG found no significant effect (trial details: general knowledge). Frontotemporal degeneration is a proteinopathy not driven by blood pressure (general knowledge). Prion-mediated dementia is unrelated to vascular risk (general knowledge). Lewy body pathology is an alpha-synucleinopathy not prevented by blood pressure control (general knowledge). Hepatic encephalopathy results from liver failure (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, prevention of cognitive decline and dementia). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nimodipine is the calcium channel blocker used after subarachnoid hemorrhage to prevent delayed ischemia, while other dihydropyridines treat hypertension, angina, and Raynaud phenomenon (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: both midlife and late-life hypertension act as a shared driver of vascular dementia and Alzheimer disease (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man comes for follow-up of stage 2 hypertension. He weighs 104 kg with a BMI of 33 kg/m², and he takes no medications. His blood pressure is 152/88 mm Hg, hemoglobin A1c is 6.1% (reference: <5.7%), and fasting LDL cholesterol is 128 mg/dL (reference: <100 mg/dL). He agrees to a supervised program to lose 10 kg over the next year. If he succeeds, about how much would his systolic pressure be expected to fall?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 mm Hg","B":"5 to 20 mm Hg","C":"2 to 4 mm Hg","D":"25 to 35 mm Hg","E":"40 to 50 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Weight reduction lowers systolic pressure by 5 to 20 mm Hg per 10 kg lost. A fall of 1 to 2 mm Hg understates the effect. A fall of 2 to 4 mm Hg also understates it. A fall of 25 to 35 mm Hg overstates it. A fall of 40 to 50 mm Hg overstates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, weight reduction). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in hypertension with asthma, ARBs, calcium channel blockers, thiazides, and cardioselective β-blockers are preferred, while nonselective β-blockers and ACE inhibitors are avoided (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hypertension treatment, p. 321). COMLEX: a first goal of losing 5% to 10% of baseline weight improves blood pressure and reduces insulin resistance (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — sarcopenia, frailty, and malnutrition","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman comes for follow-up of hypertension treated with lisinopril. She has read that potassium lowers blood pressure and asks how to increase her intake. Her blood pressure is 146/82 mm Hg, estimated GFR is 66 mL/min/1.73 m² (reference: >60 mL/min/1.73 m²), and serum potassium is 4.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). An ECG shows normal T waves. Which way of raising her potassium intake is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Potassium chloride tablets","B":"Potassium-sparing diuretic","C":"Potassium salt substitute","D":"Diet-based potassium","E":"Effervescent potassium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Potassium should come from dietary change rather than supplements to prevent hyperkalemia in patients with renal decline or those taking renin-angiotensin blockers such as lisinopril. Potassium chloride tablets are the supplement route the notes advise against. A potassium-sparing diuretic adds a second potassium-raising drug to her ACE inhibitor (general knowledge). A potassium salt substitute is a concentrated potassium chloride source (general knowledge). Effervescent potassium is another supplement form (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, potassium supplementation via diet). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral renal artery stenosis calls for caution with ACE inhibitors because they further lower GFR and can precipitate renal failure (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: raising dietary potassium to at least 4.7 g daily lowers systolic pressure by about 4 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man comes for follow-up of hypertension. He drinks four beers every evening and has no withdrawal symptoms. His blood pressure is 154/90 mm Hg, gamma-glutamyl transferase is 88 U/L (reference range: 8–61 U/L), and mean corpuscular volume is 101 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). He agrees to cut back to the level recommended for blood pressure control. What daily limit should he be advised to observe?","choices":{"A":"One drink per day","B":"Three drinks per day","C":"Two drinks per day","D":"Four drinks per day","E":"Five drinks per day"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Men should limit alcohol to no more than 2 drinks per day and women to no more than 1, which lowers systolic pressure by about 4 mm Hg; pressure rises directly at intakes of 2 or more drinks per day, so his four beers sit well above that level. One drink per day is the limit for women. Three drinks per day exceeds the limit for men. Four drinks per day is his current excess intake. Five drinks per day exceeds it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, moderation of alcohol consumption); SDL 8 notes (alcohol ≥ 2 drinks/day raises blood pressure). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: verapamil acts most on the heart and amlodipine and nifedipine most on vascular smooth muscle, with diltiazem in between (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, calcium channel blockers, p. 323). COMLEX: smoking cessation lowers blood pressure by 1 to 6 mm Hg (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia was started on doxazosin 2 weeks ago for both his hypertension and urinary symptoms. Last night he fainted on his way to the bathroom. His supine blood pressure is 148/82 mm Hg and his standing pressure is 116/70 mm Hg, and serum sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Postvoid residual volume is 60 mL (reference: <50 mL). In a large trial that compared this drug class with chlorthalidone, what additional problem led to advice against its use as primary monotherapy for hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Excess cardiac events","B":"Worse urinary retention","C":"Severe hyperkalemia","D":"Persistent dry cough","E":"Rebound hypertension"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In ALLHAT, the alpha-1 blocker arm had more cardiovascular events than the chlorthalidone arm, so these drugs should not be used as primary monotherapy for hypertension. Worse urinary retention is not expected, because alpha-1 blockade relaxes the bladder neck and prostate. Severe hyperkalemia is an adverse effect of renin-angiotensin blockers. Persistent dry cough is an ACE inhibitor effect. Rebound hypertension follows abrupt withdrawal of central alpha-2 agonists. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, peripherally acting alpha-1 receptor antagonists). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: α1-selective blockers such as prazosin, terazosin, and doxazosin treat urinary symptoms of BPH and hypertension, and prazosin is used for PTSD, with first-dose orthostatic hypotension, dizziness, and headache as adverse effects (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: alpha-1 blockers relax smooth muscle in the bladder neck and prostate (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — medication classes associated with increased fall risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman is admitted for a hip fracture repair, and all her home medications are held while she is kept without oral intake. Her home list is amlodipine, lisinopril, chlorthalidone, doxazosin, and clonidine 0.1 mg three times daily. Thirty hours later she is anxious and tremulous, her blood pressure is 214/112 mm Hg, and her pulse is 108/min. Troponin I is <0.01 ng/mL (reference: <0.04 ng/mL), and CT of the head shows no hemorrhage. Abrupt omission of which drug most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Amlodipine","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Chlorthalidone","D":"Clonidine","E":"Doxazosin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centrally acting alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine cause severe rebound hypertension when abruptly omitted. Amlodipine has a long half-life and does not cause abrupt rebound (general knowledge). Lisinopril withdrawal does not produce sympathetic rebound (general knowledge). Chlorthalidone withdrawal causes a gradual rise rather than a crisis (general knowledge). Doxazosin withdrawal does not cause this sympathetic surge (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonists). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: clonidine and guanfacine can cause CNS depression, bradycardia, hypotension, respiratory depression, and miosis (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α2-agonists, p. 243). COMLEX: central alpha-2 agonists are generally avoided in older adults because of their high risk of central nervous system toxicity (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and albuminuria comes for follow-up 3 weeks after losartan was added to his lisinopril to reduce proteinuria. He feels weak. His blood pressure is 118/64 mm Hg, serum potassium is 6.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and creatinine has risen from 1.3 to 1.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). An ECG shows peaked T waves without arrhythmia. Besides treating the hyperkalemia, which medication change is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Adding spironolactone","B":"End dual RAS blockade","C":"Doubling the losartan","D":"Adding potassium citrate","E":"Starting a DASH diet"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB is contraindicated, so one of the two renin-angiotensin blockers must be stopped. Adding spironolactone would raise potassium further (general knowledge). Doubling the losartan intensifies the cause of his hyperkalemia. Adding potassium citrate adds potassium (general knowledge). Starting a DASH diet adds potassium-rich foods. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, angiotensin II receptor blockers). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitor adverse effects include cough, angioedema, teratogenicity, a rise in creatinine, hyperkalemia, and hypotension (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: ARBs block angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor on vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal cortex, preventing vasoconstriction and aldosterone release (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — structural and functional changes of the urinary system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the obstacles resulting in low rates of successful treatment of hypertension in the elderly","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old retired widow on a fixed pension takes three antihypertensives and says she splits pills and lets refills lapse near the end of each month. She reads labels easily and has no side effects. What barrier most likely explains her poor control?","choices":{"A":"Cognitive impairment","B":"Adverse drug effects","C":"Health literacy gaps","D":"Out-of-pocket cost","E":"Visual impairment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The out-of-pocket expense of multidrug regimens on fixed retirement incomes leads patients to skip doses or leave prescriptions unfilled. Cognitive impairment causes confusion with complex dosing, which she does not describe. Adverse drug effects are absent in her account. Health literacy gaps are not suggested by her clear understanding (general knowledge). Visual impairment is excluded by her easy label reading. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 1, financial disincentives). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible α-blocker given before surgery for pheochromocytoma to prevent catecholamine-driven hypertensive crisis (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: heavy calcification of peripheral arteries can keep the cuff from compressing them and falsely raise readings in older adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in providing preventive care to older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old man has blood pressure of 172/58 mm Hg, a wide gap between his systolic and diastolic readings, and an estimated GFR that fell from 62 to 54 mL/min/1.73 m² over 2 years. Treating this pattern slows his kidney decline by limiting which process?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular hyperfiltration","B":"Immune complex formation","C":"Pulsatile vessel injury","D":"Urinary crystal deposition","E":"Tubular sodium wasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Treating isolated systolic hypertension and wide pulse pressure blunts microvascular pulsatile injury to the kidneys and slows chronic kidney disease. Glomerular hyperfiltration drives diabetic and obesity-related nephropathy (general knowledge). Immune complex formation causes glomerulonephritis unrelated to blood pressure (general knowledge). Urinary crystal deposition causes stone or crystal nephropathy (general knowledge). Tubular sodium wasting is not a consequence of systolic hypertension (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACE inhibitors dilate the efferent arteriole, lowering GFR and filtration fraction, and loss of angiotensin II feedback raises renin (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, ACE inhibitors, p. 628). COMLEX: the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline sets a systolic target below 130 mm Hg for community-dwelling, ambulatory older adults (SDL 9 notes; Ham's Ch. 36, Table 36.3).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — structural and functional changes of the urinary system with aging","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the non pharmocologic approach to the treatment of hypertension","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with hypertension has smoked a pack a day for 45 years and agrees to quit today. How soon after quitting does most of his excess cardiovascular risk fall away?","choices":{"A":"Within 5 years","B":"Within 1 month","C":"After 10 years","D":"After 15 years","E":"After 25 years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Smoking cessation cuts excess cardiovascular risk substantially within 5 years. A drop within 1 month overstates how fast the risk falls. A drop after 10 years understates the speed of benefit. A drop after 15 years understates it more. A drop after 25 years understates it further. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 3, smoking cessation). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: phentolamine is a reversible α-blocker used for hypertensive episodes in patients on MAO inhibitors who eat tyramine-rich foods and for cocaine-induced hypertension (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, α-blockers, p. 243). COMLEX: smoking acts synergistically with hypertension to raise coronary artery disease and stroke risk (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 75-year-old man develops a hot, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint 6 weeks after starting hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension. Joint fluid shows negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals. Which drug effect most likely precipitated this?","choices":{"A":"Hypokalemia","B":"Hypercalcemia","C":"Hyperuricemia","D":"Hyponatremia","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiazides can cause hyperuricemia that precipitates acute gout. Hypokalemia causes weakness and arrhythmias rather than crystal arthritis. Hypercalcemia from thiazides does not cause urate crystals (general knowledge). Hyponatremia causes confusion and falls rather than joint inflammation (general knowledge). Hypomagnesemia causes cramps and arrhythmias (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, thiazide diuretic adverse effects). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide adverse effects include hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia, hyperlipidemia, hypercalcemia, and sulfa allergy (First Aid Renal—Pharmacology, thiazide diuretics, p. 627). COMLEX: low thiazide doses rather than maximal diuretic doses are advised in older adults (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman has bilateral ankle swelling 3 weeks after amlodipine was started. Her jugular venous pressure is normal, lungs are clear, and serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). What causes the swelling?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium retention","B":"Lymphatic obstruction","C":"Bilateral venous thrombosis","D":"Reduced plasma albumin","E":"Arteriolar vasodilation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers cause pedal edema through precapillary dilation rather than volume overload. Renal sodium retention would raise the jugular venous pressure. Lymphatic obstruction has no cause in her history (general knowledge). Bilateral venous thrombosis is unlikely with symmetric drug-timed swelling (general knowledge). Reduced plasma albumin is excluded by her normal level. Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, dihydropyridine adverse effects). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydralazine is given with a β-blocker to blunt reflex tachycardia and is safe to use in pregnancy (First Aid Cardiovascular—Pharmacology, hydralazine, p. 323). COMLEX: pedal edema from calcium channel blockers is a common reason older adults stop treatment (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 5 — adherence and safety of medication use in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with uncomplicated hypertension and no heart disease asks why atenolol was not chosen as his first drug. Which finding in older adults argues against it?","choices":{"A":"Worse renal outcomes","B":"Excess stroke risk","C":"Frequent dry cough","D":"Peripheral edema","E":"Higher drug cost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta-blockers are not advised as initial monotherapy for uncomplicated hypertension at age 60 or older because of an excess risk of stroke compared with other agents. Worse renal outcomes are not the reason (general knowledge). Frequent dry cough belongs to ACE inhibitors. Peripheral edema belongs to dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers. Higher drug cost does not apply to generic atenolol (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, beta-blockers in older adults). Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: β1-selective blockers mostly have names starting A through M, nonselective ones N through Z, and carvedilol and labetalol block both α1 and β receptors (First Aid Pharmacology—Autonomic Drugs, β-blockers, p. 244). COMLEX: abrupt β-blocker cessation can trigger rebound tachycardia or acute myocardial ischemia (SDL 9 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — impact of age and multimorbidity on coronary disease outcomes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the rational for treatment of hypertesion in elderly individuals (disease prevention)","batch":5,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman comes for follow-up of untreated hypertension. Her readings at three visits average 168/62 mm Hg, and she has no heart failure, diabetes, or kidney disease. Her serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Her physician notes that the wide gap between her readings independently predicts stroke and heart failure. Large trials suggest which drug class narrows this gap most effectively?","choices":{"A":"ACE inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Calcium channel blockers","D":"Beta-blockers","E":"Alpha-1 blockers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data from large trials suggest that thiazides reduce pulse pressure more effectively than ACE inhibitors or calcium channel blockers. ACE inhibitors were less effective at this in those trials. Calcium channel blockers were also less effective. Beta-blockers are not first-line drugs for uncomplicated hypertension in older adults. Alpha-1 blockers are not advised as primary monotherapy. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Wide Pulse Pressure; the comparison is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 2, skeletal and renal protection). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs that cause hyperuricemia include pyrazinamide, thiazides, furosemide, niacin, and cyclosporine (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 249). COMLEX: coffee raises blood pressure within hours of drinking, but its long-term significance is unclear (Ham's Ch. 36, Caffeine).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — central elastic artery changes and systolic hypertension with aging","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":5,"stem":"A 73-year-old man with newly diagnosed hypertension starts chlorthalidone 12.5 mg daily. His starting blood pressure is 158/86 mm Hg, and his target is below 130/80 mm Hg. At a recheck his pressure is 150/84 mm Hg, potassium is 3.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). He reports no side effects. If the target is not reached, how soon should the dose be increased or a second drug added?","choices":{"A":"After 1 month","B":"After 1 week","C":"After 3 months","D":"After 6 months","E":"After 1 year"},"correct":"A","explanation":"If the target is not reached within 1 month of starting therapy, the initial dose should be raised or a second medication added. Waiting only 1 week acts before the drug's full effect is seen (general knowledge). Waiting 3 months delays intensification. Waiting 6 months delays it longer. Waiting 1 year prolongs exposure to uncontrolled pressure. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Overview of Medications; the 1-month interval is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, antihypertensive classes). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: drugs that cause pulmonary fibrosis include methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, carmustine, bleomycin, busulfan, and amiodarone, whereas ACE inhibitors cause a dry cough (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 250). COMLEX: in SHEP, chlorthalidone for systolic pressure of 160 mm Hg or more with diastolic pressure under 90 mm Hg reduced stroke and heart disease (Ham's Ch. 36, Isolated Systolic Hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — factors affecting drug prescribing in the elderly","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Review the classes of anti hypertensive medications, their mechanism of action and potential adverse effects","batch":5,"stem":"A 75-year-old man comes to the physician because his gums have become swollen and bleed when he brushes. Over the past year his gum tissue has grown over the lower edges of his teeth. He takes diltiazem, lisinopril, atorvastatin, metformin, and chlorthalidone, and he has no seizure disorder or transplant. His hemoglobin A1c is 6.9% (reference: <5.7%), and platelet count is 240,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which drug class in his regimen most likely explains his oral finding?","choices":{"A":"ACE inhibitor","B":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","C":"Nondihydropyridine CCB","D":"Biguanide antidiabetic","E":"Thiazide-like diuretic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nondihydropyridine agents such as diltiazem can cause gingival hyperplasia. An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril causes cough and angioedema rather than gum overgrowth. An HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor such as atorvastatin causes myalgias and liver enzyme rise (general knowledge). A biguanide antidiabetic such as metformin causes gastrointestinal upset and B12 deficiency (general knowledge). A thiazide-like diuretic such as chlorthalidone causes electrolyte and metabolic effects. Sources: Ham's Ch. 36 (Antihypertensive Drugs, nondihydropyridine adverse effects; gum overgrowth is not in the SDL 9 notes); SDL 9 lecture notes (Obj 4, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers). Textbook enrichment (Ham's) — detail not in SDL 9 lecture notes. Aligned with SDL 9 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gingival hyperplasia is a known reaction to cyclosporine, calcium channel blockers, and phenytoin (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, drug reactions, p. 249). COMLEX: no J-curve effect is seen for stroke, possibly because the heart is perfused mostly during diastole (Ham's Ch. 36, Ischemic Heart Disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 4 — adverse drug reactions in the elderly and their risk factors","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Hams"}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"About how many US adults aged 60 years and older experience some form of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"1 in 100","B":"1 in 33","C":"1 in 4","D":"1 in 10","E":"2 in 5"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Approximately 10% of US adults aged 60 years and older experience some form of mistreatment. A rate of 1 in 100 understates it. A rate of 1 in 33 also understates it. A rate of 1 in 4 overstates it. A rate of 2 in 5 is far higher than the national estimate. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, United States estimates). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abuse of children, older adults, or prisoners is one of the listed exceptions to confidentiality, along with suicidal or homicidal ideation, duty to protect, impaired drivers, and reportable diseases (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, confidentiality, p. 269). COMLEX: 1-year prevalence estimates of elder mistreatment worldwide average 14.3% (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"Among community-dwelling older adults in the United States, which subtype of elder mistreatment has the highest reported prevalence?","choices":{"A":"Financial exploitation","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Emotional mistreatment","D":"Physical mistreatment","E":"Sexual mistreatment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Financial exploitation has the highest US prevalence at 5.2%. Caregiver neglect follows closely at 5.1%. Emotional mistreatment is reported at 4.6%. Physical mistreatment is reported at 1.6%. Sexual mistreatment is a recognized subtype without a higher reported rate in the notes. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, subtype breakdown in the US). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in factitious disorder imposed on another, a caregiver causes or fabricates illness in a dependent person, most often a child but also a disabled or older adult, and it counts as child or elder abuse (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, factitious disorders, p. 583). COMLEX: in a population-based study of older Chinese Americans, about 15% reported mistreatment; only the 40.4% Hispanic figure comes from convenience samples (Ham's Ch. 33, Prevalence).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":1,"stem":"Under the US National Research Council definition, intentional harm to a vulnerable elder counts as mistreatment when the person causing it holds which kind of bond with the elder?","choices":{"A":"Blood relationship","B":"Trust relationship","C":"Legal guardianship","D":"Shared household","E":"Paid employment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The definition covers harm by a caregiver or other person who stands in a trust relationship with the elder. A blood relationship is not required. Legal guardianship is not required (general knowledge). A shared household is not part of the definition. Paid employment is not required. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, definition of elder mistreatment). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a physician determines capacity for a specific healthcare decision, whereas a judge determines competency, which covers broader legal decision-making (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: a busy primary care clinician likely sees a mistreated older adult every week (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"In the United States, which form of elder mistreatment is reported most often to the community agency that investigates elder abuse, neglect, and exploitation?","choices":{"A":"Financial exploitation","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Physical abuse","D":"Emotional abuse","E":"Self-neglect"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Self-neglect is the type reported most often to Adult Protective Services in the United States. Financial exploitation leads community prevalence surveys rather than agency reports. Caregiver neglect is reported less often. Physical abuse is reported less often. Emotional abuse is reported less often. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, epidemiological significance to APS). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: informed consent requires disclosure, understanding, capacity, and voluntariness, and patients may withdraw written consent whenever they wish, even verbally (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, informed consent, p. 268). COMLEX: recent literature increasingly treats self-neglect as a distinct geriatric syndrome (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Why is self-neglect considered a separate entity from the other forms of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"No third-party perpetrator","B":"Lower associated mortality","C":"Rarely reported to agencies","D":"Absent legal definition","E":"Younger victim profile"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Self-neglect stands apart because it is not perpetrated by a third party. A lower associated mortality is not the basis for the distinction (general knowledge). Rarely reported to agencies is wrong, since it is the most common form reported to APS. The NCEA does define it; how it is defined is not what separates it from other mistreatment. A younger victim profile is not described (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, definition and conceptualization). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neglect, the most common form of child maltreatment, presents with poor hygiene, malnutrition, withdrawn affect, and failure to thrive (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: in-home assessments are among the most effective approaches for detecting self-neglect (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is one of the five observable phenotypes of self-neglect?","choices":{"A":"Wandering","B":"Insomnia","C":"Hoarding","D":"Kleptomania","E":"Sundowning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hoarding is one of the five phenotypes of self-neglect. Wandering is a behavior seen in dementia (general knowledge). Insomnia is a sleep disorder (general knowledge). Kleptomania is an impulse-control disorder (general knowledge). Sundowning is evening agitation in dementia (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, five clinical phenotypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy brings swollen gums, easy bruising, petechiae, hemarthrosis, poor wound healing, and corkscrew hair, and a tea-and-toast diet can precipitate it (First Aid Biochemistry—Nutrition, vitamin C, p. 67). COMLEX: Black older adults experience higher rates of self-neglect and psychological mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which relationship factor is a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation of an older adult?","choices":{"A":"Long-distance caregiving","B":"Scheduled home aide visits","C":"Weekly visits from family","D":"Shared living arrangement","E":"Adult day program attendance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cohabiting with the abuser is a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation. Long-distance caregiving does not carry this risk (general knowledge). Scheduled home aide visits are not listed as a relationship factor. Weekly visits from family add social contact rather than risk (general knowledge). Adult day program attendance expands social contact (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, relationship and relational factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol use disorder causes thiamine deficiency, cirrhosis, hepatitis, pancreatitis, peripheral neuropathy, and testicular atrophy, and it is treated with naltrexone, acamprosate, or disulfiram plus support groups (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, alcohol use disorder, p. 590). COMLEX: active drug or alcohol addiction in the caregiver is a well-established perpetrator risk factor (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"A clinician weighing an older woman's risk of mistreatment looks in turn at the woman herself, at the person caring for her, at their relationship, and at her wider community and society. Organizing risk across these nested levels applies which framework?","choices":{"A":"Biomedical model","B":"Stages-of-change model","C":"Health belief model","D":"Biopsychosocial model","E":"Ecological model"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Risk factors are structured with an ecological framework spanning victim, perpetrator, relationship, community, and societal levels. The biomedical model explains illness through biological causes (general knowledge). The stages-of-change model describes readiness for behavior change (general knowledge). The health belief model explains health behavior through perceived threat and benefit (general knowledge). The biopsychosocial model combines biological, psychological, and social influences on illness (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, risk factor framework). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: physicians are ethically, and usually legally, obliged to report an impaired colleague so the colleague stops patient care and gets help (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: poverty and low socioeconomic status are community and societal risk factors for mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":1,"stem":"Which victim characteristic, whether present at baseline or newly developing, is a potent predictor of elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"Rising household income","B":"Cognitive impairment","C":"Long marital duration","D":"Regular church attendance","E":"Prior military service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Both baseline dementia and new cognitive deficits are potent predictors of elder mistreatment. Rising household income is not a risk factor, since poverty raises risk. Long marital duration is not a listed risk factor (general knowledge). Regular church attendance adds social ties, which buffer against mistreatment. Prior military service is not a listed risk factor (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, victim-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aging skin loses dermal collagen and elastin, producing wrinkles and senile purpura, and loses sweat glands, raising the risk of heat stroke (First Aid Pathology—Aging, skin, p. 225). COMLEX: severe multimorbidity, frailty, and sensory deficits increase reliance on others and limit the ability to seek help (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — Alzheimer disease pathogenesis and pathologic findings","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"An 81-year-old man with intact decision-making capacity tells his physician in Ohio that his grandson has been taking money from his bank account. He asks the physician not to tell anyone because he fears losing contact with his grandson. What is the physician's legal obligation?","choices":{"A":"Honor his request","B":"Confront the grandson","C":"File a report","D":"Defer to his family","E":"Chart it and wait"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With the exception of New York, every US state has a mandatory reporting law, so a physician in Ohio must report suspected mistreatment even when the patient asks otherwise. Honoring his request leaves a mandated report unmade. Confronting the grandson may raise the danger to the patient and is not the legal duty (general knowledge). Deferring to his family does not satisfy the physician's own duty. Charting it and waiting does not meet the reporting requirement. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, mandatory reporting). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when a patient without capacity has no advance directive, surrogates decide in the order spouse, adult children, parents, adult siblings, and other relatives (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, surrogate decision-maker, p. 269). COMLEX: reporting laws cover healthcare providers, behavioral health specialists, and social service providers (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"During a routine 15-minute visit, a primary care physician wants a validated screen for elder mistreatment that takes under 2 minutes. It should ask the patient directly about emotional, physical, and financial safety. Which tool best fits?","choices":{"A":"VASS","B":"EASI","C":"H-S/EAST","D":"MoCA","E":"PHQ-9"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elder Abuse Suspicion Index is a validated screen that takes under 2 minutes and asks the patient directly about safety, which fits a busy visit. The VASS is a longer 12-item scale of vulnerability, dependence, dejection, and coercion. The H-S/EAST measures violation of personal rights, direct abuse, and potentially abusive situations. The MoCA screens cognition rather than mistreatment (general knowledge). The PHQ-9 screens for depression (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, screening and detection tools). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: exceptions to informed consent are waiver, legal incompetence, therapeutic privilege, and emergency situations with implied consent (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, informed consent, p. 268). COMLEX: Adult Protective Services receives and investigates reports, assesses capacity, and arranges emergency shelter, medical care, and legal support (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":1,"stem":"A daughter tells her mother's physician that staff at her mother's nursing home ignore call lights, restrict visits, and dismiss her complaints about her mother's rights. No crime or Medicaid fraud is suspected, and she wants someone to advocate for her mother within the facility. Which program should the physician direct her to?","choices":{"A":"Long-Term Care Ombudsman","B":"Eldercare Locator","C":"Medicaid Fraud Control Unit","D":"Area Agency on Aging","E":"Adult day health center"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Long-Term Care Ombudsman Program, established by the Older Americans Act, advocates for the health, safety, welfare, and legal rights of residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities. The Eldercare Locator is a clearinghouse that directs callers to local agencies. A Medicaid Fraud Control Unit investigates and prosecutes Medicaid fraud and patient abuse in facilities. An Area Agency on Aging plans community services for older adults (general knowledge). An adult day health center provides daytime care (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, key reporting and support agencies). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a medical power of attorney names an agent to make decisions if the patient loses capacity, can be revoked while capacity is intact, and is more flexible than a living will (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: the Eldercare Locator (1-800-677-1116) directs clinicians and families to local adult protection agencies (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"An 86-year-old bedbound woman with advanced Parkinson disease is brought to the emergency department by a neighbor. Her son moved in 2 years ago and agreed to manage her meals, medications, and turning, but he has been away for days at a time. She is cachectic, her bedding is soiled, and she has a deep sacral wound exposing muscle with no bruises or fractures. Serum sodium is 152 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), BUN is 48 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL), and albumin is 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Her bank records show no unusual withdrawals. Which form of elder mistreatment does this most likely represent?","choices":{"A":"Caregiver neglect","B":"Self-neglect","C":"Physical mistreatment","D":"Psychological mistreatment","E":"Financial exploitation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Failure of a designated provider of care to supply food, medical necessities, and basic care needed for health and safety defines caregiver neglect. Self-neglect requires the older adult's own failure to perform self-care, not a failure by her son. Physical mistreatment requires acts intended to cause injury or pain, and she has no bruises or fractures. Psychological mistreatment involves acts causing anguish or fear, which are not described. Financial exploitation is not supported by her normal bank records. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, recognized subtypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pressure injuries are staged from non-blanchable erythema to full-thickness skin loss (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Skin, and Connective Tissue—Dermatology, cutaneous ulcers, p. 491). COMLEX: documentation should note unexplained black eyes, abrasions, burns, fractures, pressure sores, and poor hygiene (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — contributing factors that lead to frailty in the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old widow comes to her primary care physician with insomnia and constant worry for 3 months. Her adult daughter, who lives with her, calls her a burden, threatens to put her in a nursing home, and has stopped letting friends visit. She has no bruises, her finances are her own, and she eats well. Her PHQ-9 score is 12 (normal: <5), and TSH is 2.1 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which subtype of elder mistreatment best describes her daughter's behavior?","choices":{"A":"Physical mistreatment","B":"Caregiver neglect","C":"Financial exploitation","D":"Emotional mistreatment","E":"Sexual mistreatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Verbal or nonverbal acts that produce anguish, fear, or mental distress define psychological (emotional) mistreatment. Physical mistreatment requires acts intended to cause injury or pain. Caregiver neglect requires failure to provide food, shelter, or medical necessities, and she eats well. Financial exploitation is excluded because she controls her own money. Sexual mistreatment involves unwanted sexual contact, which is not described. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, recognized subtypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 80% of young adults who suffered emotional abuse as children meet criteria for at least one psychiatric illness by age 21 (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: sexual mistreatment is nonconsensual or unwanted sexual violation or contact (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman who emigrated from Guatemala 5 years ago comes to a community health center for diabetes follow-up. She lives with her nephew and speaks little English, and a staff member asks how common mistreatment is among older clients like her. Her hemoglobin A1c is 7.8% (reference: <5.7%), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.1 mg/dL). Her blood pressure is 134/80 mm Hg, and she has no injuries on examination. In convenience samples of older Hispanic immigrants, about what share report elder mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"About 5%","B":"About 15%","C":"About 40%","D":"About 25%","E":"About 60%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Convenience samples of older Hispanic immigrants show markedly high mistreatment rates, estimated at 40.4%. A share of about 5% understates it. A share of about 15% matches a population-based study of Chinese American older adults instead. A share of about 25% still understates it. A share of about 60% overstates it. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, disproportionate impact in racial and ethnic minorities). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an incapacitated patient's prior oral statements carry more weight when the patient was informed, the directive was specific, and the choice was repeated over time to several people (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: US community rates by subtype are 5.2% financial, 5.1% potential neglect, 4.6% emotional, and 1.6% physical (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man is seen at home after a neighbor reports a foul smell from his house. He lives alone, stacks of newspapers block most rooms, the kitchen has rotting food, and his water was shut off months ago. He is unkempt but oriented and denies that anyone harms him. His hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and vitamin C is 0.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.4–2.0 mg/dL). Before deciding how to intervene, what is the most important next step?","choices":{"A":"Capacity evaluation","B":"Involuntary admission","C":"Nursing home placement","D":"Police welfare check","E":"Psychiatric commitment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Evaluating his decision-making ability is essential, because it determines whether interventions stay voluntary or require coordination with APS. Involuntary admission overrides his choices before his capacity is known. Nursing home placement is premature without knowing whether he can decide for himself. A police welfare check adds nothing, since he has already been seen at home (general knowledge). Psychiatric commitment requires danger from a mental illness, which has not been established (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide from furnaces, space heaters, and motor exhaust is odorless and causes headache, vomiting, confusion, and visual disturbances, often in several household members at once (First Aid Respiratory—Physiology, cyanide vs carbon monoxide poisoning, p. 689). COMLEX: other phenotypes include severe impairment in personal hygiene and a house in need of critical repair (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — risk factors for poor nutritional status in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman is visited at home after her pharmacist notices she has not refilled her medications. Her home is cluttered and dirty, her refrigerator is empty, and she has lost 6 kg over 4 months. She understands her situation, weighs the risks, and clearly declines to leave her home, and her MoCA score is 27/30 (normal: ≥26/30). Her albumin is 3.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Court-ordered guardianship","B":"Voluntary support services","C":"Involuntary hospitalization","D":"Mandatory day program","E":"Mandatory home removal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With intact decision-making capacity, interventions focus on voluntary concrete services such as referrals, meal delivery, case management, counseling, and home health assistance. Court-ordered guardianship is reserved for patients who have lost capacity. Involuntary hospitalization overrides a capable patient's choice (general knowledge). A mandatory day program overrides a capable adult's choice (general knowledge). Mandatory home removal also overrides her capable refusal (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intellectual disability or mental illness does not exclude capacity unless the condition currently impairs the ability to make the healthcare decision (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: APS receives more reports of self-neglect than of any other mistreatment type in the United States (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — benefits of shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 88-year-old man with moderate Alzheimer disease lives alone and has stopped paying his bills, and his electricity and heat have been shut off. He cannot describe his situation or the risks of staying, and his MoCA score is 11/30 (normal: ≥26/30). He has no family, no advance directive, and no one holding power of attorney. His core temperature is 35.4 °C (reference range: 36.5–37.5 °C), and serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L). Besides immediate medical care, which measure should the clinician pursue with the community protective agency?","choices":{"A":"Hospice enrollment","B":"Driving evaluation","C":"Respite care","D":"Hearing screening","E":"Conservatorship"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When decision-making capacity is lost, clinicians coordinate with APS on conservatorship, guardianship, or financial management assistance to preserve safety. Hospice enrollment does not address his loss of capacity or safety (general knowledge). A driving evaluation does not address the unpaid bills and unsafe home (general knowledge). Respite care supports a caregiver, and he has none (general knowledge). Hearing screening does not address his safety (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, clinical assessment and management). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: decisions a patient made while capacity was intact stay valid even if the patient later loses capacity (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, decision-making capacity, p. 267). COMLEX: the definition of elder mistreatment also includes a caregiver's failure to meet an elder's basic needs or to protect the elder from harm (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — benefits of shared decision making with the elderly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman comes to the clinic with bruises on both upper arms. Her 52-year-old son lost his job a year ago and now relies on her pension and savings to pay his rent and bills. He comes to every visit and becomes angry when she mentions cutting off the money. Hemoglobin is 12.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and platelet count is 230,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which perpetrator-specific risk factor does the son's situation illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Neighborhood poverty","B":"Psychiatric illness history","C":"Dependency on the victim","D":"Severe victim frailty","E":"Active alcohol misuse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The caregiver's financial, housing, or emotional reliance on the older victim is a perpetrator-specific risk factor. Neighborhood poverty is a community-level factor rather than a perpetrator-specific one. A psychiatric illness history is not suggested by the vignette. Severe victim frailty is a victim-level factor. Active alcohol misuse is not suggested by the vignette. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, perpetrator-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonaccidental injuries often show differing stages of healing or shapes resembling the implement used (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: shared living arrangements are a strong independent risk factor for physical abuse and financial exploitation (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old widow moved across the country a year ago to be near her nephew, who now handles her money. She has no friends in the new city, attends no community groups, and talks only to her nephew. She has lost interest in leaving her apartment. Her examination is unremarkable, her BMI is 23 kg/m², and her TSH is 1.8 μU/mL (reference range: 0.5–4.5 μU/mL). Which community-level factor most increases her risk of mistreatment?","choices":{"A":"Low social support","B":"Functional dependence","C":"Abuser dependency","D":"Shared living arrangement","E":"Cognitive impairment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low levels of support and poor embeddedness in community networks significantly raise mistreatment risk. Functional dependence is a victim-level factor. Abuser dependency is a perpetrator-level factor. A shared living arrangement is a relationship-level factor. Cognitive impairment is a victim-level factor. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, community and societal factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: confidentiality may be broken when potential physical harm to the patient or others is serious and imminent, alternative ways to protect those at risk are not possible, and steps can be taken to prevent the harm (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, confidentiality, p. 269). COMLEX: minority status raises vulnerability to financial exploitation through socioeconomic inequities and systemic barriers (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — domains of management in disease prevention in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman brings her 81-year-old father with advanced dementia to the clinic and says she looks after him about 70 hours a week while working part time. She has had no break since taking on his care a year ago, sleeps about 4 hours a night, and says the workload has left her overwhelmed and at the end of her rope. She admits she recently shouted at him and shoved him into a chair. His examination shows a faint bruise on the forearm, and his hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which perpetrator risk factor best explains her behavior?","choices":{"A":"Abuser dependency","B":"Caregiver burnout","C":"Substance use","D":"Psychiatric illness","E":"Prior family conflict"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Overwhelmed, highly stressed providers of care are at substantial risk of becoming abusive or neglectful. Abuser dependency is not suggested, since nothing indicates she relies on him for money, housing, or emotional support. Substance use is not suggested by the vignette. Psychiatric illness is not suggested by the vignette. Prior family conflict is not described. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, perpetrator-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: burnout is prolonged, excessive stress that leads to emotional exhaustion and depersonalization and reduces professional efficacy (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Healthcare Delivery, burnout and fatigue, p. 277). COMLEX: caregiver neglect is the failure of a designated caregiver to provide essential care, food, shelter, clothing, or medical necessities (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department by her son after a fall at home. She has bruises of different colors on both upper arms, and her son answers every question for her and insists on staying in the room. Radiographs show a healing rib fracture that was never reported. Her hemoglobin is 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and INR is 1.0 (reference range: 0.8–1.2). What is the most appropriate first step?","choices":{"A":"Confront the son","B":"Await proof of abuse","C":"Discharge with the son","D":"Notify her pastor","E":"Private interview"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The immediate priority is her physical safety, which begins with speaking to the older adult privately without the caregiver present. Confronting the son may escalate the danger to her (general knowledge). Awaiting proof of abuse delays protection, and proof is not required. Discharge with the son returns her to a possibly unsafe home. Notifying her pastor breaches confidentiality without improving her safety (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, prioritize victim safety). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: for a victim of intimate partner violence, ask whether the patient is safe and help make an emergency plan, without necessarily pressing the patient to leave or to report unless state law requires it (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: the H-S/EAST measures violation of personal rights, direct abuse, and potentially abusive situations (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — complete assessment of the patient with fall risk or falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man with mild dementia has an unexplained burn on his forearm, and his daughter's account of it changes between visits. He is calm and cannot recall how it happened. The physician is unsure whether harm occurred and asks whether she must first be certain before contacting the state agency. His albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and his hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). What standard triggers the duty to report?","choices":{"A":"Confirmed injury pattern","B":"Reasonable suspicion","C":"Patient's written consent","D":"Police confirmation","E":"Witnessed assault"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A reasonable, good-faith suspicion is legally sufficient, and clinicians need not prove mistreatment before reporting. A confirmed injury pattern is not required. The patient's written consent is not required for a mandated report. Police confirmation is not required. A witnessed assault is not required. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, no requirement for definitive proof). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when a dependent patient's injuries do not match the caretaker's story, document a detailed history and examination, interview the patient alone if possible, give needed care, and inform the caretaker of the duty to report (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: state agencies conduct on-site investigations to substantiate reported findings (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman is admitted with a hip fracture, facial bruises, and dehydration, and her bank reports that a caregiver forged checks on her account. The hospital convenes geriatrics, social work, nursing, psychiatry, a lawyer, and a forensic specialist to plan her care. Her serum sodium is 149 mEq/L (reference range: 136–145 mEq/L), and BUN is 38 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). She is alert and asks what will happen next. Which resource is this group, which coordinates emergency intervention and possible prosecution?","choices":{"A":"Hospital ethics committee","B":"Ombudsman program","C":"Risk management office","D":"Multidisciplinary team","E":"Nurse Aide Registry"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Specialized hospital and community groups that combine medicine, social work, nursing, psychiatry, law, and forensic specialists coordinate emergency interventions and prosecutions. A hospital ethics committee advises on ethical dilemmas rather than prosecution (general knowledge). The ombudsman program advocates for residents of long-term care facilities. A risk management office protects the institution from liability (general knowledge). The Nurse Aide Registry keeps abusive aides from being rehired. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, law enforcement and multidisciplinary teams). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a do-not-resuscitate order prohibits CPR, but the patient may still choose other life-sustaining measures such as intubation, feeding tubes, or chemotherapy (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, advance directives, p. 268). COMLEX: State survey agencies investigate aides and report substantiated abuse to the MFCU; the state keeps the Nurse Aide Registry (Ham's Ch. 33, Table 33.3).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the definitionand types of elder mistreatment as well as the prevalence of this problem","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old Black man asks whether older adults in his community face a higher risk of having their money misused by others. Compared with other groups, how much higher can that risk be?","choices":{"A":"Up to 1.2-fold","B":"Up to 3.8-fold","C":"Up to 1.9-fold","D":"Up to 6.5-fold","E":"Up to 10-fold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Black older adults experience up to a 3.8-fold increased risk of financial exploitation relative to other groups. A figure of up to 1.2-fold understates it. A figure of up to 1.9-fold also understates it. A figure of up to 6.5-fold overstates it. A figure of up to 10-fold overstates it further. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 1, disproportionate impact in racial and ethnic minorities). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide poisoning is treated with 100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen (First Aid Pharmacology—Toxicities and Adverse Effects, specific toxicity treatments, p. 247). COMLEX: the global community prevalence of elder mistreatment is about 15.7%, roughly 141 million older adults (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — challenges in preventive care in older adults","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize self neglect as a form of elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"An 80-year-old man's home is tidy and structurally sound, and he is well groomed, but his electricity, heat, and water were shut off for nonpayment. Which self-neglect phenotype does he show?","choices":{"A":"Poor personal hygiene","B":"Unsanitary living conditions","C":"Critical home disrepair","D":"Inadequate utilities","E":"Hoarding behavior"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lack of running water, heating, or electricity is the inadequate utilities phenotype of self-neglect. Poor personal hygiene is excluded, since he is well groomed. Unsanitary living conditions are excluded by his tidy home. Critical home disrepair is excluded, since the house is structurally sound. Hoarding behavior is excluded by the lack of clutter. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 2, five clinical phenotypes). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbon monoxide raises carboxyhemoglobin, which co-oximetry detects but pulse oximetry does not, and it is classically linked to bilateral globus pallidus lesions on MRI (First Aid Respiratory—Physiology, cyanide vs carbon monoxide poisoning, p. 689). COMLEX: when capacity is lost, clinicians work with APS on guardianship or financial management assistance (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman with long-standing major depression lives with her grandson. Which category of risk factor for elder mistreatment does her depression represent?","choices":{"A":"Victim factor","B":"Perpetrator factor","C":"Relationship factor","D":"Community factor","E":"Societal factor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Baseline depression, mental illness, or substance abuse history in the older adult is a victim-specific risk factor. A perpetrator factor would describe the grandson, not her. A relationship factor describes interactions such as cohabitation or past conflict. A community factor describes social support and networks. A societal factor describes poverty or systemic inequities. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, victim-specific risk factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: geriatric patients lose phase I drug metabolism first, while phase II is relatively preserved (First Aid Pharmacology—Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics, drug metabolism, p. 230). COMLEX: inability to perform activities of daily living raises the risk of physical, psychological, and financial mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 3 — the geriatric syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the risk factors for elder mistreatment","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman's husband, who hit her repeatedly during their 50-year marriage, has become her sole provider of care. Which relationship-level risk factor does this history represent?","choices":{"A":"Caregiver burnout","B":"Past spousal violence","C":"Financial dependency on her","D":"Severe victim frailty","E":"Low social support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A history of domestic violence or dysfunctional family dynamics is a relationship-level risk factor. Caregiver burnout is a perpetrator factor, and no overload is described. Financial dependency on her is a perpetrator factor not described here. Severe victim frailty is a victim-level factor. Low social support is a community-level factor. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 3, relationship and relational factors). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sexual abuse of children most often leaves no physical signs and peaks at ages 9 to 12 years (First Aid Psychiatry—Pathology, child abuse, p. 573). COMLEX: strong social ties act as a protective buffer against mistreatment (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — coordinated care management and caregivers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":3,"stem":"A clinician screens an 80-year-old woman with the Elder Abuse Suspicion Index, and she answers no to all five patient questions. The clinician notices she is underweight, unwashed, and has unexplained bruises on her arms. How should these findings be handled within the screen?","choices":{"A":"Discard as nonspecific","B":"Repeat the questions later","C":"Score the observation item","D":"Ask her caregiver","E":"Accept her negative answers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The EASI includes one clinician-observation question about signs such as malnutrition, poor hygiene, or bruises, so these findings count even when the patient denies problems. Discarding them as nonspecific ignores the item built to capture them. Repeating the questions later delays action on signs already seen (general knowledge). Asking her caregiver can expose the patient to a possible abuser (general knowledge). Accepting her negative answers overlooks the observed signs. Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, screening and detection tools). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: if surrogates indicate the patient would not have wanted ventilator support, it is withheld or withdrawn regardless of the surrogate's own preference (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Ethics, ventilator-assisted life support, p. 269). COMLEX: APS assesses capacity and arranges emergency shelter, medical care, and legal support (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — goals and challenges of chronic disease management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the responsibility, tools available, and agencies available to report concerns for elder mistreatment (no need to memorize)","batch":3,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman has a black eye and a wrist fracture, and her caregiver's account of the fall differs from hers. Besides the physical findings, what should the physician document?","choices":{"A":"Clinician's opinion of guilt","B":"Family's insurance details","C":"Caregiver's criminal record","D":"Discrepancies in the history","E":"Abuser's employment history"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Records should capture physical findings and discrepancies in the history. The clinician's opinion of guilt is a legal judgment outside the medical record (general knowledge). The family's insurance details do not document mistreatment (general knowledge). The caregiver's criminal record is not the clinician's to establish (general knowledge). The abuser's employment history is not part of the clinical record (general knowledge). Sources: SDL 10 lecture notes (Obj 4, documentation). Aligned with SDL 10 lecture notes (2026-11-22).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when an invasive test is done on the wrong patient, the physician must tell the patient about the mistake regardless of the outcome (First Aid Public Health Sciences—Communication Skills, challenging scenarios, p. 273). COMLEX: the immediate priority when mistreatment is suspected is the patient's physical safety (SDL 10 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — complete assessment of the patient with fall risk or falls","isHighYield":true}]};
